{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-963e8aa5c3cc11ac", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t\n2\t\n3\tMusicLayout: Explicit Structural Planning for Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n4\t\n5\t \n6\t \n7\t\n8\t \n9\t Title:\n10\t \n11\t\n12\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n13\t\n14\t Description:\n15\t \n16\t \n17\t\n18\t \n19\t \n20\t\n21\t \n22\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n23\t Learn more\n24\t ×\n25\t \n26\t\n27\t \n28\t License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n29\t \n30\t \n31\tarXiv:2608.09035v1 [cs.SD] 10 Aug 2026\n32\t\n33\tMusicLayout: Explicit Structural Planning\n34\tfor Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n35\t\n36\tShuyu Li1\n37\t\n38\t  \n39\tKejun Zhang1,3\n40\tCorresponding author.\n41\t  \n42\tJiahe Lei4\n43\t\n44\t  \n45\tShulei Ji2,3\n46\t\n47\t  \n48\tZihao Wang2,5\n49\t\n50\t  \n51\tJiaxing Yu1\n52\t\n53\t  \n54\tWanying Wu6\n55\t\n56\t  \n57\tLei Wang7\n58\t\n59\t1College of Artificial Intelligence, Zhejiang University\n60\t2College of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang University\n61\t3Innovation Center of Yangtze River Delta, Zhejiang University\n62\t4The Chinese University of Hong Kong\n63\t5Shandong University\n64\t6Chu Kochen Honors College, Zhejiang University\n65\t7Ant Group\n66\t\n67\t{lsyxary, zhangkejun, shuleiji, yujx, 3240100265}@zju.edu.cn\n68\t1155261729@ee.cuhk.edu.hk, carlwang1212@gmail.com, thirtyking@163.com\n69\t\n70\tAbstract\n71\tText-to-music generation has advanced rapidly, but current systems still rely primarily on global text prompts, leaving the structural organization of generated music implicit and difficult to inspect, control, or revise before audio generation. To address this issue, we introduce MusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for controlling musical structure in text-to-music generation. MusicLayout describes a musical piece as a time-aligned layout of sections, textures, repetitions, variations, and instrument-level arrangements, serving as an interpretable planning layer between textual intent and the generated music. We integrate MusicLayout into a text-to-music framework built on a unified autoregressive formulation, where the model first generates a MusicLayout representation and subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned on this representation within a single sequence. The resulting MusicLayout can be inspected and modified prior to audio generation, providing a mechanism for layout-level structural control. We evaluate MusicLayout through layout-conditioned generation, layout manipulation experiments, and matched-data ablations, providing evidence that explicit layout planning can improve long-range structural organization and support layout-level control.\n72\t\n73\t1 Introduction\n74\t\n75\tRecent text-to-music models have advanced substantially in fidelity,\n76\tsemantic alignment, musicality, and efficiency [19]. They\n77\ttypically generate discrete audio tokens autoregressively or synthesize\n78\tcontinuous and compressed representations with diffusion models\n79\t[1, 4, 20]. Recent\n80\tsystems further support long-form generation and combine language-model (LM)\n81\treasoning with diffusion-based acoustic synthesis\n82\t[10, 13].\n83\t\n84\tDespite these advances, most systems rely on global text prompts and leave\n85\tmusical planning implicit. A prompt can describe genre, mood,\n86\tinstrumentation, or tempo, but cannot precisely specify how sections are\n87\torganized, materials recur or vary, textures evolve, or instruments enter\n88\tand leave. Existing controllable methods add melody, chords, drums,\n89\tdynamics, symbolic lead sheets, or semantic representations\n90\t[39, 26, 34, 2, 13]. These\n91\tcontrols are often limited to individual attributes, require additional\n92\tmusical inputs, or remain implicit. They do not expose a unified,\n93\ttime-aligned representation for specifying the organization and arrangement\n94\tof an entire piece.\n95\t\n96\tExplicit planning representations improve controllability and\n97\tinterpretability in image generation by exposing high-level organization\n98\tbefore content. PlanGen [14], for example, generates spatial\n99\tlayout tokens before image tokens in one autoregressive sequence. For music,\n100\tthis principle could improve long-range structural organization and\n101\tcontrollability. Transferring it requires representing organization along\n102\ttime rather than space.\n103\t\n104\tFocusing on instrumental music, we introduce\n105\tMusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for planning\n106\tmusical structure in audio language models. It describes\n107\tsection transitions, repetitions and variations, texture changes, and\n108\tinstrument participation along a shared timeline. As illustrated in\n109\tFigure 1, given a text prompt, our model generates a\n110\tMusicLayout representation and subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned\n111\ton it within a single sequence. This makes structural\n112\tplanning inspectable before synthesis and supplies audio generation with an\n113\texplicit description of how the music should unfold. Experiments on\n114\tlayout conditioning and manipulation provide evidence that MusicLayout can\n115\timprove structural organization and support layout-level control.\n116\t\n117\tFigure 1: Conventional autoregressive LM-based text-to-audio generation (top)\n118\tand our explicit-planning framework (bottom). Our LM generates MusicLayout\n119\ttokens and then discrete audio tokens, labeled audio codes in the figure,\n120\twithin one sequence. A frozen decoder renders the audio tokens into music.\n121\tThe abbreviated rewrite selects the musical section at 49–55 seconds,\n122\tassigns it the chorus label, and sets its active instrument list to\n123\tdrums. The <roles> tag delimits that list.\n124\t\n125\tOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n126\t\n127\t• \n128\t\n129\tWe propose MusicLayout, an explicit planning\n130\trepresentation for unified audio language models. It represents music\n131\tas a time-aligned layout of sections, repetitions, variations,\n132\ttextures, and instrument arrangements, providing structured musical\n133\tplans beyond global textual prompts.\n134\t\n135\t• \n136\t\n137\tWe develop a unified autoregressive framework for\n138\tlayout planning and audio generation, where a single model\n139\tfirst produces an explicit MusicLayout plan and then continues to\n140\tgenerate audio tokens conditioned on the preceding layout within a single\n141\tsequence. By making musical planning explicit, this framework\n142\tsupports long-range structural organization and layout-level control.\n143\t\n144\t• \n145\t\n146\tThrough evaluations of layout-conditioned generation,\n147\tlayout manipulation, and matched-data ablations, we provide evidence\n148\tthat MusicLayout can serve as an interpretable planning interface and\n149\timprove long-range structural organization in text-to-music generation.\n150\t\n151\t2 Related Work\n152\t\n153\t2.1 Text-to-Music Generation\n154\t\n155\tAutoregressive music models predict discrete audio tokens, as exemplified by\n156\tJukebox, MusicLM, and MusicGen\n157\t[6, 1, 4].\n158\tDiffusion-based systems instead synthesize waveforms, spectrograms, or\n159\tcompressed latents through iterative denoising\n160\t[16, 31, 23].\n161\tAudioLDM 2 and MeLoDy combine LM-based semantic modeling with diffusion\n162\tsynthesis [20, 18]. Stable Audio 3 supports\n163\tefficient variable-length generation, while ACE-Step 1.5 uses a hybrid\n164\tarchitecture in which an LM performs high-level planning and a Diffusion\n165\tTransformer (DiT) realizes the audio [10, 13, 28]. Although\n166\tsome systems incorporate high-level planning, temporal organization and\n167\tarrangement remain implicit or coarsely represented.\n168\t\n169\t2.2 Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n170\t\n171\tPrior systems supplement global prompts with melody references, rhythm,\n172\tdynamics, chords, drums, or other symbolic and audio conditions\n173\t[1, 4, 39, 26, 34].\n174\tSeed-Music uses a symbolic lead-sheet pipeline [2]. ACE-Step 1.5 uses metadata and\n175\tsong-blueprint planning, but its blueprint remains implicit in the learned\n176\taudio-token representations and is not exposed as an interpretable structural plan\n177\t[13]. Other methods use individual controls,\n178\tadditional inputs, task-specific modules, or coarse metadata. MusicLayout\n179\tinstead exposes sections, textures, repetitions, variations, and instrument\n180\tarrangements in an inspectable and adjustable time-aligned plan.\n181\t\n182\t3 Methodology\n183\t\n184\tWe build our model upon ACE-Step 1.5, extending its LM to generate a\n185\tMusicLayout before producing layout-conditioned audio tokens. The original\n186\taudio tokenizer and DiT remain frozen, with the latter rendering the tokens\n187\tinto a waveform.\n188\t\n189\tFigure 2: A MusicLayout example aligned to a shared timeline. Rows show sections,\n190\tmaterial families, textures, variations, and instrument arrangements. Family\n191\tcolors identify recurring material, and instrument entries give register,\n192\tdensity, and energy. Blanks denote inactivity. For readability, rhythm, light, and\n193\tstrong abbreviate rhythm_driven, light_var, and\n194\tstrong_var, respectively.\n195\t\n196\t3.1 MusicLayout Representation\n197\t\n198\tMusicLayout externalizes temporal musical organization as a structured,\n199\thuman-readable sequence that bridges high-level structural intent and\n200\taudio-token generation. It follows three design principles: temporal\n201\talignment, hierarchical organization, and controllability.\n202\t\n203\tA MusicLayout combines piece-level structural relationships among musical\n204\tmaterials with time-aligned segments, each corresponding to one musical\n205\tsection.\n206\tAt the piece level, MusicLayout organizes recurring musical materials\n207\tand their relationships, including repetitions and variations across\n208\tsections. At the segment level, each segment is associated with a\n209\ttime-aligned span and describes its section identity, texture evolution,\n210\tand instrument participation.\n211\tIts functional section label is selected from intro, verse,\n212\tprechorus, chorus, bridge, breakdown,\n213\toutro, transition, hook, solo, and\n214\tbuild.\n215\tFigure 2 illustrates these attributes along a\n216\tshared timeline.\n217\tMusicLayout is serialized as discrete tokens, allowing it to be directly\n218\tmodeled by an autoregressive LM.\n219\t\n220\tA family is a piece-local group of segments that share similar musical\n221\tmaterial. Labels such as fam_0 and fam_1 identify families\n222\tonly within the current piece. The same label may denote different musical\n223\tmaterial in another piece. Each segment is marked\n224\tas unique, same, light_var, or\n225\tstrong_var. These values indicate, respectively, that the segment\n226\thas no repeated family, closely repeats its family pattern, or departs from\n227\tthat pattern to a smaller or larger degree. Thus, the family colors and the\n228\tvariation row in Figure 2 jointly describe which\n229\tmaterial returns and how much it changes.\n230\t\n231\tCategory\n232\t\n233\tMeaning\n234\t\n235\tlayered\n236\t\n237\tCoexisting lead and supporting layers\n238\t\n239\trhythm_driven\n240\t\n241\tRhythm-section-centered arrangement\n242\t\n243\tmelodic_front\n244\t\n245\tForegrounded melodic material\n246\t\n247\tharmonic_bed\n248\t\n249\tSustained harmonic support\n250\t\n251\tpercussive\n252\t\n253\tPercussion-centered material\n254\t\n255\tbuild_up\n256\t\n257\tAccumulating or intensifying layers\n258\t\n259\tsparse_pulse\n260\t\n261\tSparse intermittent activity\n262\t\n263\tlead_front\n264\t\n265\tForegrounded lead material\n266\t\n267\tcontrast\n268\t\n269\tDeliberately contrasting texture\n270\t\n271\tother\n272\t\n273\tCases outside the listed categories\n274\t\n275\tTable 1: Texture categories in MusicLayout.\n276\t\n277\tThe texture field gives a coarse description of how a musical segment is\n278\torganized. Table 1 defines its vocabulary. Each\n279\tsegment contains a <roles> block listing its active instruments.\n280\tFigures 1 and 2 use the\n281\tform instrument:register:density:energy,\n282\tfor example drums:low:sparse:med.\n283\tThe instrument field uses a predefined vocabulary of 25 categories. Each\n284\tinstrument is followed by register\n285\t(low/mid/high), density\n286\t(sparse/med/dense), and energy\n287\t(low/med/high), encoding pitch range, activity, and\n288\tintensity, respectively.\n289\tFigure 1 abbreviates the edited field as drums.\n290\t\n291\tWe derived MusicLayout annotations from symbolic representations by extracting\n292\ttemporal sections, repetitions, variations, textures, and instrument\n293\tarrangements. We rendered the symbolic music into audio and aligned each layout\n294\twith its audio and text prompt to form prompt–layout–audio training triples.\n295\t\n296\t3.2 Unified Layout Planning and Audio Generation\n297\t\n298\tModeling.\n299\tGiven conditioning context , the LM serializes MusicLayout sequence \n300\tbefore audio-token sequence within a single autoregressive sequence:\n301\t\n302\t(1)\n303\t\n304\twhere <layout> and </layout> delimit the planning region.\n305\tThis ordering makes layout generation precede and condition audio-token\n306\tgeneration. The corresponding joint distribution factorizes as\n307\t\n308\t(2)\n309\t\n310\tHere, describes layout planning from the conditioning context,\n311\twhile describes audio-token generation given the context and\n312\tlayout.\n313\t\n314\tTraining.\n315\tWe train the LM on two complementary next-token tasks. Layout planning trains\n316\tthe model to generate from . Layout-conditioned audio generation trains\n317\tit to predict from and a ground-truth (GT) MusicLayout:\n318\t\n319\t(3)\n320\t\n321\tWe initially optimize layout planning to learn the MusicLayout schema and the\n322\tschema-specific special tokens used to mark its fields and boundaries. We then\n323\talternate the tasks, applying the loss only to the\n324\tcorresponding layout or audio target span. Only the LM is updated. The\n325\tpretrained audio tokenizer and DiT synthesis components remain frozen.\n326\t\n327\tInference and control.\n328\tIn automatic inference, the LM generates a MusicLayout from the conditioning\n329\tcontext and then continues with audio tokens. Alternatively, the human-readable\n330\tlayout can be inspected and adjusted before audio generation to change temporal\n331\torganization or instrument arrangement. The revised layout and original\n332\tcontext form the autoregressive prefix for audio-token generation, providing a\n333\tpre-synthesis interface for layout-level control.\n334\t\n335\t4 Experiments\n336\t\n337\tWe evaluate generation, structural control, layout manipulation, ablations,\n338\tand subjective quality against representative text-to-music systems.\n339\tFor our framework, we consider two layout conditions. In the reference-layout\n340\tcondition, the model receives a MusicLayout derived from the target music. This\n341\tcondition jointly tests whether MusicLayout captures the target’s musical\n342\torganization and whether the model can use it to control the structure of the\n343\tgenerated audio. In the generated-layout condition, the model predicts a layout\n344\tfrom the text prompt before generating audio, evaluating the complete\n345\tend-to-end text-to-music process.\n346\t\n347\t4.1 Experimental Setup\n348\t\n349\tDatasets.\n350\t\n351\tWe used three datasets with complementary roles. FreeMIDI [12]\n352\tprovided the training data and a disjoint in-domain evaluation set.\n353\tMidiCaps [24] was used to evaluate generalization to\n354\tout-of-domain MIDI, while MuChin [38] provided an out-of-domain\n355\tevaluation on real audio. Their details are summarized in\n356\tTable 2.\n357\t\n358\tDataset\n359\tAudio source\n360\tTrain\n361\tEval.\n362\t\n363\tFreeMIDI\n364\tMIDI-synth.\n365\t24,474\n366\t2,719\n367\t\n368\tMidiCaps\n369\tMIDI-synth.\n370\t–\n371\t1,040\n372\t\n373\tMuChin\n374\tReal, separated accomp.\n375\t–\n376\t1,000\n377\t\n378\tTable 2: Datasets used in our experiments.\n379\t\n380\tFreeMIDI.\n381\tWe collected a subset of FreeMIDI and retained only pieces longer than 15 seconds.\n382\tFor each remaining MIDI file, we extracted a MusicLayout and synthesized its\n383\taudio, forming a layout–audio pair. We then\n384\tprovided the audio to MOSS-Music [27], an audio-understanding language model, to produce\n385\tthe generation prompt, yielding aligned prompt–layout–audio triples.\n386\t\n387\tMidiCaps.\n388\tMidiCaps already provides natural-language captions. We selected a subset with\n389\ta balanced distribution of style labels and removed MIDI files that overlap\n390\twith FreeMIDI. For each remaining piece, we extracted a layout and synthesized\n391\taudio from the MIDI, directly pairing both with its caption to form evaluation\n392\ttriples.\n393\t\n394\tMuChin.\n395\tMuChin contains vocal music with Chinese captions but no MIDI, and thus cannot\n396\tsupport the reference-layout condition. We used DeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] to translate the captions into\n397\tEnglish and remove vocal-related descriptions. We further separated the\n398\taccompaniment from the vocals using a community-trained BS-RoFormer\n399\tmodel [22] and\n400\tused only the separated accompaniment for metric computation. Generation\n401\tconditions that require an externally supplied layout—the\n402\treference-layout and shuffled-layout-inference conditions—are not reported\n403\ton MuChin.\n404\t\n405\tCompared systems.\n406\t\n407\tWe compared against MusicGen [4], ACE-Step 1.5 and\n408\tACE-Step XL-Turbo [13], and Stable Audio 3 Medium\n409\t[10]. We evaluated our model under either the\n410\tgenerated-layout or the reference-layout condition. To control for the effect of additional\n411\ttraining data, we also finetuned ACE-Step 1.5 on the same data using the same LM\n412\tadaptation setup as our model. We denote this matched-data no-layout control as\n413\tACE-Step 1.5-FT. It directly predicts audio from the conditioning context \n414\twithout MusicLayout tokens, serving as both the primary fair comparison for explicit layout planning and\n415\tthe first step of the progressive ablation. Systems shared evaluation items,\n416\tprompts, and seeds when supported, and baselines used officially recommended\n417\tdecoding settings. Systems without a layout representation\n418\ttargeted each item’s reference duration.\n419\tFor long-form MusicGen generation, we followed its\n420\tofficial continuation mechanism.\n421\tFor our model, the generation\n422\tduration is instead determined by the end time of the conditioning layout. In\n423\tthe generated-layout condition, this duration is therefore predicted by the\n424\tmodel rather than specified externally, whereas the reference-layout\n425\tcondition matches the reference duration because its layout is derived from\n426\tthe target music.\n427\t\n428\tEvaluation protocol.\n429\t\n430\tWe report Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD) [30] for acoustic\n431\tdistribution similarity, PaSST-KL [17] for sound-event\n432\tagreement, SSIM [37] for local time–frequency similarity,\n433\tand CLAPScore [7] for text–audio alignment.\n434\t\n435\tFor long-range structure, SCM Energy Distance\n436\t[5, 33] compares corpus-level structural\n437\tcomplexity distributions, while and acoustic-boundary\n438\tagreement [36] measure fine- and coarse-grained\n439\ttransition alignment within each piece.\n440\tTo examine how layout manipulation changes long-range organization, we also\n441\tvisualize recurrence-based self-similarity matrices (SSMs), following the\n442\tstructural visualization used in Stable Audio 2 and its underlying music\n443\tstructure analysis method [9, 32].\n444\t\n445\tImplementation details.\n446\t\n447\tWe finetuned ACE-Step 1.5’s 1.7B-parameter LM using six A800 GPUs, a\n448\t4,096-token context, and an effective batch size of 48. We selected the\n449\tcheckpoint with the lowest development layout-to-audio loss. Layout and audio\n450\tdecoding used\n451\ttemperature/top- values of and .\n452\t\n453\t4.2 Results\n454\t\n455\tOverall Generation Performance\n456\t\n457\tDataset\n458\tGroup\n459\tSystem\n460\t\n461\tFAD\n462\t\n463\tKL\n464\t\n465\tSSIM\n466\t\n467\tCLAP\n468\t\n469\tSCM\n470\t\n471\tFreeMIDI\n472\tBaselines\n473\tACE-Step 1.5\n474\t2.874\n475\t0.789\n476\t0.159\n477\t0.284\n478\t0.117\n479\t0.637\n480\t0.831\n481\t\n482\tACE-Step XL-Turbo\n483\t2.584\n484\t0.734\n485\t0.181\n486\t0.300\n487\t0.063\n488\t0.619\n489\t0.811\n490\t\n491\tMusicGen-Large\n492\t3.706\n493\t0.834\n494\t0.189\n495\t0.280\n496\t0.375\n497\t0.558\n498\t0.757\n499\t\n500\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n501\t3.014\n502\t0.838\n503\t0.213\n504\t0.378\n505\t0.181\n506\t0.616\n507\t0.809\n508\t\n509\tOur model\n510\tReference layout\n511\t2.610\n512\t0.740\n513\t0.183\n514\t0.238\n515\t0.054\n516\t0.645\n517\t0.838\n518\t\n519\tGenerated layout\n520\t2.495\n521\t0.719\n522\t0.153\n523\t0.241\n524\t0.206\n525\t0.643\n526\t0.834\n527\t\n528\tMatched-data\n529\tACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout)\n530\t3.345\n531\t0.956\n532\t0.154\n533\t0.219\n534\t0.380\n535\t0.577\n536\t0.739\n537\t\n538\tcontrols\n539\tShuffled-layout training\n540\t2.756\n541\t0.721\n542\t0.153\n543\t0.216\n544\t0.100\n545\t0.607\n546\t0.810\n547\t\n548\tShuffled-layout inference\n549\t2.636\n550\t0.779\n551\t0.176\n552\t0.230\n553\t0.038\n554\t0.642\n555\t0.831\n556\t\n557\tMidiCaps\n558\tBaselines\n559\tACE-Step 1.5\n560\t2.526\n561\t0.797\n562\t0.152\n563\t0.290\n564\t0.110\n565\t0.632\n566\t0.828\n567\t\n568\tACE-Step XL-Turbo\n569\t3.036\n570\t0.756\n571\t0.174\n572\t0.313\n573\t0.100\n574\t0.606\n575\t0.801\n576\t\n577\tMusicGen-Large\n578\t2.488\n579\t0.825\n580\t0.180\n581\t0.258\n582\t0.379\n583\t0.545\n584\t0.746\n585\t\n586\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n587\t2.123\n588\t0.726\n589\t0.229\n590\t0.352\n591\t0.315\n592\t0.616\n593\t0.812\n594\t\n595\tOur model\n596\tReference layout\n597\t2.149\n598\t0.712\n599\t0.169\n600\t0.305\n601\t0.087\n602\t0.635\n603\t0.836\n604\t\n605\tGenerated layout\n606\t2.303\n607\t0.699\n608\t0.140\n609\t0.287\n610\t0.804\n611\t0.630\n612\t0.830\n613\t\n614\tMatched-data\n615\tACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout)\n616\t2.740\n617\t0.877\n618\t0.153\n619\t0.234\n620\t0.351\n621\t0.560\n622\t0.736\n623\t\n624\tcontrols\n625\tShuffled-layout training\n626\t2.772\n627\t0.734\n628\t0.144\n629\t0.267\n630\t0.136\n631\t0.599\n632\t0.807\n633\t\n634\tShuffled-layout inference\n635\t2.192\n636\t0.740\n637\t0.165\n638\t0.293\n639\t0.117\n640\t0.635\n641\t0.834\n642\t\n643\tMuChin\n644\tBaselines\n645\tACE-Step 1.5\n646\t2.480\n647\t0.685\n648\t0.129\n649\t0.289\n650\t0.186\n651\t0.588\n652\t0.797\n653\t\n654\tACE-Step XL-Turbo\n655\t1.994\n656\t0.617\n657\t0.147\n658\t0.317\n659\t0.611\n660\t0.566\n661\t0.779\n662\t\n663\tMusicGen-Large\n664\t3.279\n665\t0.717\n666\t0.156\n667\t0.241\n668\t0.215\n669\t0.517\n670\t0.756\n671\t\n672\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n673\t2.007\n674\t0.662\n675\t0.177\n676\t0.365\n677\t0.132\n678\t0.573\n679\t0.794\n680\t\n681\tOur model\n682\tGenerated layout\n683\t3.456\n684\t0.671\n685\t0.116\n686\t0.285\n687\t0.655\n688\t0.594\n689\t0.809\n690\t\n691\tMatched-data\n692\tACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout)\n693\t3.124\n694\t0.844\n695\t0.123\n696\t0.222\n697\t1.458\n698\t0.535\n699\t0.726\n700\t\n701\tcontrols\n702\tShuffled-layout training\n703\t3.714\n704\t0.670\n705\t0.120\n706\t0.286\n707\t0.626\n708\t0.600\n709\t0.820\n710\t\n711\tTable 3: Objective results. CLAP, KL, and SCM abbreviate CLAPScore, PaSST-KL,\n712\tand SCM Energy Distance. Bold and underline mark the first- and second-ranked\n713\tnumerical values per dataset, with rounded ties bold. The three matched-data\n714\tcontrols remove MusicLayout or mismatch it in training or inference.\n715\t\n716\tTable 3 summarizes the objective results. No single\n717\tsystem dominates all aspects of generation.\n718\tOur model with generated layouts records the lowest FAD and PaSST-KL on\n719\tFreeMIDI. On MidiCaps, it also records the lowest PaSST-KL, suggesting that\n720\tlayout-conditioned generation can remain close to the reference acoustic-event\n721\tdistribution despite out-of-domain MIDI captions. Its lower CLAPScore relative\n722\tto Stable Audio and ACE-Step XL-Turbo, however, shows that explicit planning\n723\tmay not uniformly improve global text–audio correspondence.\n724\t\n725\tThe matched-data no-layout control provides a more direct test of the\n726\tcontribution of explicit layout planning. It is weaker than the original\n727\tACE-Step 1.5 on nearly all metrics. This degradation\n728\tlikely reflects the training-data difference: the original model was trained\n729\ton high-quality real recordings, whereas our finetuning data consists of\n730\tMIDI-synthesized audio with lower audio quality and timbral fidelity, as well\n731\tas a different acoustic distribution. Since our model and this control share\n732\tthe same finetuning data and LM adaptation setup, their comparison isolates\n733\tthe effect of explicit layout planning. Relative to this control, the\n734\treference-layout condition has more favorable values on all seven metrics for\n735\tFreeMIDI and MidiCaps. The generated-layout condition does so on six metrics\n736\tfor FreeMIDI and five for MidiCaps and MuChin.\n737\tThese\n738\tresults show that explicit layout planning substantially improves generation under matched-data\n739\tconditions and can partly offset the limitations of weaker training audio.\n740\t\n741\tOverall, our model is competitive on the two MIDI-derived datasets,\n742\tindicating that explicit layout planning contributes to overall music\n743\tgeneration performance. Its FAD, PaSST-KL, and SSIM results on MuChin are\n744\tless favorable, however. This difference likely arises from an acoustic-domain\n745\tshift: our model is trained on MIDI-synthesized instrumental audio, whereas\n746\tMuChin consists of accompaniments separated from real recordings.\n747\t\n748\tStructural Control\n749\t\n750\tThe reference-layout condition records the highest boundary scores shown for\n751\tFreeMIDI, the highest MidiCaps , and a tie for the highest MidiCaps\n752\t.\n753\tBecause the reference layouts are derived from the target music, these results\n754\tprovide evidence that MusicLayout captures target organization and that\n755\tconditioning on it can guide corresponding structure in the generated audio.\n756\t\n757\tWith model-generated layouts, our model also shows strong structural\n758\torganization without access to the target-derived layout used in the\n759\treference-layout condition. On FreeMIDI, both boundary scores are numerically\n760\tabove all baselines. On MidiCaps, is numerically above all baselines,\n761\twhile remains competitive. On MuChin, both boundary scores are\n762\tnumerically above all baselines, showing that this advantage persists under an\n763\tacoustic-domain shift from MIDI-synthesized training data to accompaniments\n764\tseparated from real recordings. These results\n765\tindicate that explicit intermediate planning improves the organization of\n766\tlong-range musical structure in end-to-end generation.\n767\t\n768\tThe comparison with the matched-data no-layout control further isolates this\n769\tstructural benefit. Both layout conditions have higher boundary scores than\n770\tthe no-layout control wherever available, and the generated-layout condition\n771\tretains this advantage across all three datasets.\n772\tThus, under the same MIDI-synthesized training data, explicit layout planning\n773\timproves boundary agreement in both controlled and automatic generation.\n774\t\n775\tSCM Energy Distance measures agreement between dataset-level structural\n776\tcomplexity distributions rather than song-level layout correctness. On the\n777\ttwo MIDI-derived evaluation sets, our model in the reference-layout condition\n778\trecords the lowest observed SCM Energy Distance among the non-ablation systems\n779\t, indicating that the specified layouts are realized with corpus-level\n780\tstructural complexity close to the references.\n781\tOn MuChin,\n782\tthe SCM Energy\n783\tDistance in the generated-layout condition is comparatively high. This weaker agreement is consistent with the\n784\ttraining–evaluation\n785\tshift from MIDI-synthesized instrumental music to accompaniments separated\n786\tfrom real vocal recordings, which have substantially different\n787\tstructural-complexity distributions. However, with training data controlled,\n788\tthe generated-layout condition has lower SCM than the no-layout condition.\n789\tThis comparison suggests a structural benefit from layout planning.\n790\t\n791\tFigure 3: Structural effects of layout manipulation for two FreeMIDI and two\n792\tMidiCaps examples. Each row presents, from left to right, the target GT under\n793\tPrompt A and Layout A, generation under the same prompt and reference layout,\n794\tthe layout-donor GT under Prompt B and Layout B,\n795\tand generation from Prompt A paired with Layout B. For clear visualization,\n796\tSSM intensities are normalized independently within each panel. Darker regions\n797\twithin a panel indicate stronger recurrence. Audio signals in each row are\n798\tcropped to their common minimum duration.\n799\t\n800\tStructural Effects of Layout Manipulation\n801\t\n802\tFigure 3 presents four separate\n803\tlayout-manipulation cases, with two drawn from FreeMIDI and two from MidiCaps.\n804\tThese cases examine how layout manipulation changes the generated structure\n805\twhile the text prompt is held fixed. In each\n806\trow, item A serves as the target and provides Prompt A and its reference Layout\n807\tA. A different item B supplies Layout B, which we refer to as the donor layout.\n808\tThe SSM for item B is included to visualize the recurrence structure associated\n809\twith Layout B. We refer to generation from Prompt A and Layout A as the matched\n810\tgeneration, and generation from the same prompt and Layout B as the manipulated\n811\tgeneration. In these examples, the matched generation\n812\tshows recurrence patterns similar to the target reference, while the\n813\tmanipulated generation loses some target-aligned patterns and exhibits patterns\n814\tcloser to the donor reference. Because the manipulated generation combines the\n815\ttarget prompt with the donor layout, it can retain structural characteristics\n816\tassociated with the target while adopting aspects of the donor’s organization.\n817\tTogether, these cases illustrate that MusicLayout can be manipulated before\n818\taudio generation to control the structure of the generated music.\n819\t\n820\tFine-Grained Regional Controls\n821\t\n822\tWe further tested three direct edits to a single 240-second MusicLayout,\n823\tholding the text prompt and sampling seeds fixed so that each controlled\n824\tcondition differed from the original only in the declared MusicLayout fields.\n825\tFigure 4 compares the original and\n826\tcontrolled audio over the affected interval.\n827\t\n828\tFirst, in the 0–11-second introduction, we replaced sparse, high-energy\n829\tstrings and synthesizer strings with dense, high-energy drums and sparse,\n830\thigh-energy bass. This edit was expected to add stronger low-frequency rhythmic\n831\tcontent and broadband percussive transients. The controlled spectrogram shows\n832\tboth effects: energy below 250 Hz becomes dominant and repeated vertical\n833\ttransients appear across the band.\n834\t\n835\tSecond, over 142–196 seconds, we changed every active role to sparse density\n836\tand medium energy while leaving the segment boundaries, labels, families, and\n837\tinstrument identities unchanged. The expected result was a less dense and\n838\tless intense realization of the same region. Relative to the original, the\n839\tcontrolled excerpt has visibly reduced broadband activity and its RMS level\n840\tdecreases by 15% (1.4 dB).\n841\t\n842\tThird, we preserved the complete 0–91-second prefix and rewrote the remaining\n843\tform from a largely repeated chorus sequence into a breakdown, bridge, build,\n844\tsolo, chorus, and outro, together with their associated roles and recurrence\n845\tfamilies. The expected result was a new long-range progression after the\n846\tpreserved prefix. The controlled spectrogram exhibits the intended succession\n847\tof contrasting regimes, including the reduced texture of the breakdown and\n848\tbridge, the subsequent build, and the denser solo and chorus.\n849\tThus, all three edits produce changes consistent with their specified local or\n850\tregional controls.\n851\t\n852\tFigure 4: Paired log-mel spectrograms for three fine-grained MusicLayout\n853\tcontrols: (a) role replacement over 0–11 seconds, (b) reduced density and\n854\tenergy over 142–196 seconds, and (c) a form rewrite over 91–240 seconds.\n855\tLight-blue boxes use matched time–frequency coordinates: A1/A2 mark\n856\tlow-frequency and broadband changes, B marks reduced broadband activity, and\n857\tC1–C3 mark successive stages of the form rewrite. Each pair shares its 0-dB\n858\treference and 80-dB range. Intensities are comparable within a row. Time axes\n859\tretain full-song coordinates.\n860\t\n861\tAblation Study\n862\t\n863\tThe three matched-data controls in Table 3 form a\n864\tprogressive ablation of MusicLayout. First, the finetuned ACE-Step 1.5 control\n865\tremoves MusicLayout entirely. Second, shuffled-layout training introduces\n866\tMusicLayout but pairs each training item with a layout from another item, so\n867\tthe model learns from mismatched layout–audio pairs. At inference, this model\n868\tgenerates its own layout from the prompt before audio generation, following the\n869\tsame end-to-end procedure as the full generated-layout condition. Third, shuffled-layout\n870\tinference restores correctly paired layouts during training but replaces the\n871\tconditioning layout with one from another item at inference.\n872\t\n873\tShuffled-layout training has favorable values on five of seven metrics relative to the no-layout control for\n874\teach of the three datasets. This pattern suggests that\n875\tlayout-shaped supervision may retain\n876\ta generic structural benefit even when item-level information is incorrect.\n877\tOn the two MIDI-synthesized datasets, our full model with generated layouts\n878\thas favorable values on five of seven metrics relative to shuffled-layout training for both\n879\tFreeMIDI and MidiCaps. Because both conditions generate layouts at inference,\n880\tthis pattern suggests that correct layout–audio correspondence during training\n881\tprovides information beyond the presence of layout-shaped tokens and can\n882\timprove long-form music generation when the evaluation domain matches the type\n883\tof audio used for training. On MuChin, the two conditions obtain broadly similar\n884\tresults. This may result from the acoustic-domain shift discussed above, since our\n885\tmodel was trained on MIDI-synthesized audio rather than accompaniments from\n886\treal recordings.\n887\t\n888\tHolding correctly paired training fixed, the reference-layout condition has favorable\n889\tFAD, KL, SSIM, CLAPScore, and values relative to shuffled-layout inference on both MIDI-derived\n890\tdatasets. This indicates that item-matched layouts provide a more informative\n891\tinference-time conditioning signal than a plausible but unrelated layout.\n892\t\n893\tA shuffled layout remains musically plausible because it comes from a real\n894\tpiece and retains valid sections, timing, repetitions, and arrangements.\n895\tShuffling can therefore preserve the corpus-level layout distribution while\n896\tbreaking item-level correspondence, so low SCM alone does not demonstrate\n897\tcorrect layout control.\n898\t\n899\t4.3 Subjective Evaluation\n900\t\n901\tAll ()\n902\t\n903\tExp. ()\n904\t\n905\tSystem\n906\tF\n907\tM\n908\tT\n909\tF\n910\tM\n911\tT\n912\t\n913\tGT\n914\t3.200\n915\t3.490\n916\t3.408\n917\t3.000\n918\t3.361\n919\t3.300\n920\t\n921\tACE 1.5\n922\t3.283\n923\t3.329\n924\t3.225\n925\t3.031\n926\t3.342\n927\t3.108\n928\t\n929\tACE-Step 1.5-FT\n930\t2.931\n931\t2.632\n932\t2.702\n933\t2.631\n934\t2.386\n935\t2.661\n936\t\n937\tOurs-Ref\n938\t3.064\n939\t3.012\n940\t3.093\n941\t2.989\n942\t2.917\n943\t3.183\n944\t\n945\tOurs-Gen\n946\t2.914\n947\t2.586\n948\t2.869\n949\t2.733\n950\t2.783\n951\t2.839\n952\t\n953\tTable 4: Subjective ratings on FreeMIDI and MidiCaps (0–5). F/M/T\n954\tdenote fidelity, musical impression, and text consistency. Exp. denotes\n955\texperienced listeners, and FT denotes matched-data finetuning.\n956\t\n957\tSystem\n958\tScore\n959\t\n960\tACE-Step 1.5\n961\t2.319\n962\t\n963\tACE-Step 1.5-FT\n964\t1.444\n965\t\n966\tOurs (reference layout)\n967\t2.556\n968\t\n969\tOurs (generated layout)\n970\t2.044\n971\t\n972\tTable 5: GT-reference structural similarity rated by\n973\tmusic-experienced participants on the same MIDI-derived items (0–5).\n974\t\n975\tTo enable a direct comparison with the reference-layout condition, we excluded\n976\tMuChin because it does not provide reference layouts. We sampled 10 items each\n977\tfrom FreeMIDI and MidiCaps and assigned them repeatedly to 27 participants,\n978\tincluding 15 with prior music experience. Participants rated audio fidelity,\n979\tmusical impression, and text consistency on a 0–5 scale, with one decimal\n980\tplace allowed. Each evaluation compared all five systems,\n981\tyielding 59 ratings per system, including 36 ratings from experienced\n982\tlisteners.\n983\t\n984\tTable 4 shows that comparisons with the matched-data\n985\tno-layout control vary with layout condition and listener experience. Across\n986\tall participants, the reference-layout condition has higher observed scores on\n987\tall three dimensions. Generated-layout scores are broadly comparable,\n988\twith similar fidelity and musical impression but higher text consistency.\n989\tWithin the experienced subset, both layout conditions score numerically higher\n990\tthan the control across all dimensions. Notably, the reference-layout condition\n991\talso has higher observed text consistency than the original ACE-Step 1.5 among\n992\texperienced listeners (3.183 vs. 3.108), suggesting that an explicit\n993\tMusicLayout can provide an additional control signal for steering music\n994\tgeneration. This pattern may arise because\n995\tmusic-experienced listeners are more attentive to structural organization when\n996\tevaluating generated music. ACE-Step 1.5 has the highest observed fidelity\n997\tscore across the five systems (3.283), above GT (3.200). As discussed above,\n998\tthis difference is consistent with the acoustic-quality gap between the\n999\tMIDI-synthesized data in our experiments and the real recordings used to train\n1000\tACE-Step 1.5. It\n1001\tunderscores the need for matched-data controls to\n1002\tisolate layout planning from training-data differences.\n1003\t\n1004\tUsing the same dataset scope, we conducted a follow-up on a subset of the\n1005\tpreviously sampled items. A subset of the experienced participants was shown\n1006\tthe GT reference for each item and asked to rate the structural\n1007\tsimilarity of the remaining outputs to the references. The reference-layout\n1008\tcondition received a higher observed structural\n1009\tsimilarity than ACE-Step 1.5, providing subjective evidence that MusicLayout\n1010\tcaptures reference organization and carries it into generated audio. The\n1011\tgenerated-layout condition was slightly weaker because coarse dataset prompts\n1012\tdo not uniquely specify the GT layout. Once predicted, the layout steers\n1013\tgeneration toward its own plausible structure. The objective structural\n1014\tmetrics above suggest that these outputs can remain structurally organized even\n1015\twhen they do not reproduce the particular GT structure.\n1016\t\n1017\t5 Conclusion\n1018\t\n1019\tWe presented MusicLayout, an explicit, time-aligned representation of section\n1020\torganization, development, and instrument arrangement for a unified\n1021\tautoregressive audio LM. Generated before audio tokens, it makes structural\n1022\tplanning inspectable and adjustable. The same LM plans the layout and predicts\n1023\taudio tokens conditioned on it while the synthesis components remain frozen.\n1024\tOur results suggest that MusicLayout provides an interpretable interface that\n1025\tsupports long-range structural organization and layout-level control.\n1026\t\n1027\tDespite these benefits, MusicLayout adjusts structural plans only before\n1028\tsynthesis and cannot edit existing audio or regenerate selected regions.\n1029\tOur reliance on MIDI-synthesized training audio may also limit audio fidelity.\n1030\tFuture work will pursue finer control and higher-quality, structurally aligned\n1031\taudio data.\n1032\t\n1033\tAcknowledgments\n1034\t\n1035\tThis work was supported by Ant Group.\n1036\t\n1037\tAppendix A MusicLayout Representation and Annotation\n1038\t\n1039\tA.1 Serialization Grammar\n1040\t\n1041\tMusicLayout is serialized as a discrete text sequence with one optional\n1042\tpiece-level family block and one required segment block. A family records a\n1043\tpiece-local identifier, its member segments, its cardinality, and whether the\n1044\tmembers are adjacent. Each segment records an integer-second time span, a\n1045\tfunctional section label, a texture, an arrangement-change label, its family\n1046\tmembership and family role, the degree of variation from the family prototype,\n1047\ta repetition flag, and a list of active instrument tuples. The grammar used in\n1048\tall reported experiments is summarized below:\n1049\t\n1050\t<layout>\n1051\t<families>\n1052\t<fam> id=fam_K members=sI,sJ count=N adj=true|false </fam>\n1053\t</families>\n1054\t<segments>\n1055\t<seg> id=sI time=A-B label=L texture=T change=C\n1056\t fam=fam_K fam_role=R variation=V repeat=true|false\n1057\t<roles> instrument:register:density:energy | ... </roles>\n1058\t</seg>\n1059\t</segments>\n1060\t</layout>\n1061\t\n1062\tFamily identifiers are local to a piece rather than global semantic labels.\n1063\tFor example, fam_0 denotes one recurring material within the current\n1064\tpiece and may refer to unrelated material in another piece. The extraction\n1065\tpipeline retains singleton material as an explicitly declared one-member\n1066\tfamily with count=1 and adj=true. Its segment uses\n1067\tvariation=unique and repeat=false. For a recurring family,\n1068\tthe first chronological member serves as its variation prototype and is marked\n1069\tsame. Later members are marked same, light_var, or\n1070\tstrong_var according to their distance from that prototype.\n1071\t\n1072\tA.2 Closed Vocabularies\n1073\t\n1074\tTable 6 gives the structural vocabularies.\n1075\tThe change field describes the segment’s arrangement-level relation\n1076\tto its predecessor, whereas fam_role describes its role in the\n1077\tpiece-level recurrence organization. These fields are distinct from the\n1078\tfunctional section label.\n1079\t\n1080\tField\n1081\t\n1082\tAllowed values\n1083\t\n1084\tSection label\n1085\t\n1086\tintro, verse, prechorus,\n1087\tchorus, bridge, breakdown, outro,\n1088\ttransition, hook, solo, build\n1089\t\n1090\tTexture\n1091\t\n1092\tlayered, rhythm_driven,\n1093\tmelodic_front, harmonic_bed, percussive,\n1094\tbuild_up, sparse_pulse, lead_front,\n1095\tcontrast, other\n1096\t\n1097\tChange\n1098\t\n1099\tentry, continuation, lift,\n1100\tdrop, contrast, outro\n1101\t\n1102\tFamily role\n1103\t\n1104\tintro_anchor, primary_repeat,\n1105\tsecondary_repeat, adjacent_variant,\n1106\toutro_anchor, unique\n1107\t\n1108\tVariation\n1109\t\n1110\tunique, same, light_var,\n1111\tstrong_var\n1112\t\n1113\tRegister\n1114\t\n1115\tlow, mid, high\n1116\t\n1117\tDensity\n1118\t\n1119\tsparse, med, dense\n1120\t\n1121\tEnergy\n1122\t\n1123\tlow, med, high\n1124\t\n1125\tTable 6: Closed structural and arrangement vocabularies in MusicLayout.\n1126\t\n1127\tThe instrument field uses 25 compact categories:\n1128\tacoustic_piano, electric_piano,\n1129\tplucked_keyboard, mallet_bell, organ,\n1130\taccordion_harmonica, acoustic_guitar,\n1131\telectric_guitar, bass, strings,\n1132\torchestral, synth_strings, choir_voice,\n1133\tbrass, sax, woodwind,\n1134\tsynth_lead, synth_pad, synth_fx,\n1135\tworld_plucked, percussion,\n1136\treverse_cymbal, sound_fx, drums, and\n1137\tother. General MIDI program numbers are mapped deterministically to\n1138\tthese categories. Instrument entries retain the segment-local register,\n1139\tdensity, and energy attributes. These attributes are defined at the instrument\n1140\ttuple level rather than duplicated as separate segment-level fields.\n1141\t\n1142\tA.3 Variation Strength\n1143\t\n1144\tFor each recurring family, we compare every later segment with the first family\n1145\tmember. Let be the Jaccard distance between active-role sets, and let\n1146\t, , and be normalized differences in energy, note density, and\n1147\tactive-role count. The base distance is\n1148\t\n1149\t(4)\n1150\t\n1151\tThe implementation adds small deterministic adjustments when coarse energy,\n1152\tdensity, texture, arrangement-change, repeat-neighbor, or source-relation\n1153\tevidence differs from the family prototype. We serialize scores at most \n1154\tas same, scores in as light_var, and larger\n1155\tscores as strong_var. This field\n1156\ttherefore records an interpretable, rule-derived degree of variation rather\n1157\tthan a human rating.\n1158\t\n1159\tA.4 Validation\n1160\t\n1161\tBefore a generated layout can condition audio decoding, the parser checks the\n1162\touter layout and segment wrappers, unique segment identifiers, nondecreasing\n1163\tinteger-second start times, positive spans, valid closed-vocabulary values,\n1164\tfour-field instrument tuples, family-member counts, and references from\n1165\tsegments to declared families. It also rejects legacy bar spans, legacy role\n1166\ttags, segment-level energy/density fields, internal MIDI program slugs, and\n1167\taudio-code tokens inside the layout. A failed layout is not silently repaired\n1168\tor rewritten.\n1169\t\n1170\tA.5 Annotation Pipeline\n1171\t\n1172\tThe annotation pipeline begins from aligned MIDI and rendered WAV files. It\n1173\textracts bar-level activity and instrument statistics, combines change and\n1174\trepetition evidence into time-aligned segments, groups structurally related\n1175\tsegments into piece-local material families, and derives the section, texture,\n1176\tchange, family, variation, and instrument attributes serialized above. MIDI\n1177\tprograms are used only as annotation evidence. The learning target remains\n1178\taudio-token generation. Segment spans are converted to audio time and rounded\n1179\tto integer seconds, with a minimum duration of one second after rounding.\n1180\t\n1181\tThe extraction procedure is deterministic once its feature and boundary\n1182\tsettings are fixed. Algorithm 1 summarizes its\n1183\texecution order.\n1184\tThe symbolic representation supplies the structural evidence. The paired\n1185\twaveform is used to place the resulting spans on the rendered-audio timeline.\n1186\t\n1187\tAlgorithm 1 MusicLayout extraction from aligned symbolic music and audio\n1188\t\n1189\t1:MIDI performance , aligned waveform duration , and fixed extraction settings \n1190\t\n1191\t2:Valid serialized layout , or rejection symbol \n1192\t\n1193\t3:function ExtractMusicLayout()\n1194\t\n1195\t4:   notes, programs, MIDI duration, bars, beats\n1196\t\n1197\t5:  for do\n1198\t\n1199\t6:    density, energy, pitch, and six broad-role activities\n1200\t\n1201\t7:  end for\n1202\t\n1203\t8:  \n1204\t\n1205\t9:  \n1206\t\n1207\t10:  \n1208\t\n1209\t11:  \n1210\t\n1211\t12:  \n1212\t\n1213\t13:   adjacent-repeat and local-change tests\n1214\t\n1215\t14:  \n1216\t\n1217\t15:  \n1218\t\n1219\t16:  \n1220\t\n1221\t17:  \n1222\t\n1223\t18:  for do\n1224\t\n1225\t19:   \n1226\t\n1227\t20:   \n1228\t\n1229\t21:  end for\n1230\t\n1231\t22:  \n1232\t\n1233\t23:  for do chronological greedy centroid assignment\n1234\t\n1235\t24:   \n1236\t\n1237\t25:   if or then\n1238\t\n1239\t26:     \n1240\t\n1241\t27:   else\n1242\t\n1243\t28:     \n1244\t\n1245\t29:   end if\n1246\t\n1247\t30:  end for\n1248\t\n1249\t31:  for do\n1250\t\n1251\t32:    relation evidence, not family assignment\n1252\t\n1253\t33:  end for\n1254\t\n1255\t34:  \n1256\t\n1257\t35:  for do\n1258\t\n1259\t36:   \n1260\t\n1261\t37:   for do\n1262\t\n1263\t38:     \n1264\t\n1265\t39:   end for\n1266\t\n1267\t40:  end for\n1268\t\n1269\t41:  \n1270\t\n1271\t42:  for do\n1272\t\n1273\t43:   \n1274\t\n1275\t44:  end for\n1276\t\n1277\t45:  \n1278\t\n1279\t46:  if then\n1280\t\n1281\t47:   return \n1282\t\n1283\t48:  end if\n1284\t\n1285\t49:  return \n1286\t\n1287\t50:end function\n1288\t\n1289\tAppendix B Dataset Construction and Provenance\n1290\t\n1291\tB.1 FreeMIDI\n1292\t\n1293\tFreeMIDI [12] supplies the training corpus and an in-domain evaluation set. We\n1294\tretained pieces longer than 15 seconds, extracted one MusicLayout from each\n1295\tretained MIDI file, and synthesized the aligned audio at 44.1 kHz using the\n1296\tFluidSynth [11] interface provided by\n1297\tPrettyMIDI [29], with\n1298\tMuseScore_General.sf2 [25]. Of 27,237\n1299\textracted items, 27,229 received a full-song caption from\n1300\tMOSS-Music-8B-Instruct [27]. Caption decoding was greedy. The prompt requested one\n1301\tconcise English paragraph grounded only in the audio, covering style, audible\n1302\tinstrumentation and roles, rhythm, texture, energy, musical development, and\n1303\tmood. It explicitly prohibited the use of filenames, MIDI metadata, external\n1304\tlabels, bullet lists, and unsupported claims.\n1305\t\n1306\tRequiring at least 15 seconds of audio and a nonempty supervised audio-code\n1307\tspan retained 27,198 items. Five additional items were removed because their\n1308\tcomplete planning sequences exceeded the 4,096-token context. The resulting\n1309\t27,193 items were split deterministically into 24,474 training and 2,719\n1310\tdevelopment items with seed 20260501. The development split is also the\n1311\tin-domain evaluation set.\n1312\t\n1313\tB.2 MidiCaps\n1314\t\n1315\tMidiCaps [24] is an out-of-domain MIDI evaluation\n1316\tset. We first sampled 1,100 items\n1317\twith seed 42 after requiring a duration of at least 120 seconds, at least two\n1318\tinstruments, and a nonempty caption. Genre-quota sampling followed by\n1319\tinstrument-diverse greedy selection covered 40 source genre labels. Because\n1320\tthe labels are multi-valued, their frequencies need not sum to the number of\n1321\titems. The most frequent labels in the final set are electronic (581), pop\n1322\t(463), rock (208), classical (108), soundtrack (102), ambient (81), and jazz\n1323\t(59). Exact SHA-256 comparison of the MIDI files against the complete FreeMIDI\n1324\tcorpus removed 60 overlaps, leaving 1,040 unique items. The source location and\n1325\tMIDI SHA-256 jointly define item identity. We then extracted MusicLayouts and\n1326\tsynthesized aligned audio using the same renderer as for FreeMIDI. Original\n1327\tMidiCaps captions were retained as prompts, and no MidiCaps item was used for\n1328\ttraining.\n1329\t\n1330\tB.3 MuChin\n1331\t\n1332\tMuChin [38] provides an out-of-domain real-audio evaluation. Because it does not\n1333\tprovide MIDI, it cannot supply a reference MusicLayout. We constructed a fixed\n1334\t1,000-item manifest in the source metadata order. For each item,\n1335\tDeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] received the original full-song\n1336\tdescription as context and a\n1337\tset of non-vocal tags covering instrumentation, arrangement, style, tempo,\n1338\trhythm, and mood. The instruction required one concise English paragraph using\n1339\tonly these safe musical attributes and explicitly prohibited any mention or\n1340\timplication of singers, vocals, lyrics, rap, choir, humming, or spoken dialogue.\n1341\tDecoding used temperature 0.2 and top- 0.9. We appended the same explicit\n1342\tinstrumental-only control phrase to every final prompt.\n1343\t\n1344\tWe separated accompaniment from vocals with the Python toolkit\n1345\taudio-separator v0.44.3 [3] and the\n1346\tBS-RoFormer [22] Viperx-1297 checkpoint [35],\n1347\tdistributed as\n1348\tmodel_bs_roformer_ep_317_sdr_12.9755.ckpt.\n1349\tWe used the resulting accompaniment as the metric reference.\n1350\tReference-layout and inference-time shuffled-reference-layout conditions are\n1351\tconsequently not applicable to MuChin.\n1352\t\n1353\tDataset\n1354\tTrain\n1355\tEvaluation\n1356\tRef. layout\n1357\t\n1358\tFreeMIDI\n1359\t24,474\n1360\t2,719\n1361\tYes\n1362\t\n1363\tMidiCaps\n1364\t0\n1365\t1,040\n1366\tYes\n1367\t\n1368\tMuChin\n1369\t0\n1370\t1,000\n1371\tNo\n1372\t\n1373\tTable 7: Dataset roles and final item counts.\n1374\t\n1375\tAppendix C Model Adaptation and Training\n1376\t\n1377\tC.1 Training Sequences and Loss Masks\n1378\t\n1379\tThe adapted model uses the 1.7B-parameter LM from\n1380\tACE-Step 1.5 [13]. Layout planning\n1381\tand audio-token prediction share the same LM but use separate supervised target\n1382\tspans. In the planning task, the prompt and chat context are inputs and the\n1383\tMusicLayout tokens are targets. In the layout-to-audio task, the prompt and\n1384\tground-truth MusicLayout form the input prefix and only the following audio-code\n1385\ttokens are targets. Chain-of-thought metadata is retained in the serialized\n1386\tprefix but receives zero loss weight. The pretrained 5-Hz audio tokenizer and\n1387\tDiT renderer [28] remain frozen in all stages.\n1388\t\n1389\tStage 1 trains only the newly introduced vocabulary rows in the input\n1390\tembedding and LM head. Old vocabulary rows are masked, and no transformer layer\n1391\tor final normalization parameter is updated. Stage 2 updates the LM while\n1392\talternating the planning and layout-to-audio tasks. The development\n1393\tlayout-to-audio loss determines checkpoint selection.\n1394\t\n1395\tSetting\n1396\t\n1397\tStage 1\n1398\t\n1399\tStage 2\n1400\t\n1401\tTasks\n1402\t\n1403\tLayout planning\n1404\t\n1405\tLayout planning and layout-to-audio\n1406\t\n1407\tEpoch limit\n1408\t\n1409\t10\n1410\t\n1411\t100\n1412\t\n1413\tLearning rate\n1414\t\n1415\tMonitored development task\n1416\t\n1417\tPlanning\n1418\t\n1419\tLayout-to-audio\n1420\t\n1421\tEarly-stopping patience\n1422\t\n1423\t5\n1424\t\n1425\t10\n1426\t\n1427\tTrainable scope\n1428\t\n1429\tNew embedding/LM-head rows\n1430\t\n1431\tLM\n1432\t\n1433\tLoss-bearing targets\n1434\t\n1435\tLayout span\n1436\t\n1437\tLayout or audio span for the sampled task\n1438\t\n1439\tTable 8: Stage-specific optimization protocol. The best Stage-2 checkpoint is\n1440\trestored according to development layout-to-audio loss.\n1441\t\n1442\tTraining used six 80-GB GPUs, a maximum sequence length of 4,096, per-rank\n1443\tbatch size 2, gradient accumulation 4, and effective batch size 48. Parameters\n1444\tand AdamW [21] optimizer states remained FP32, while\n1445\tFSDP [41] computation used BF16.\n1446\tFull-shard FSDP used original parameters, automatic wrapping, synchronized\n1447\tmodule states, limited all-gathers, and replicated vocabulary parameters for\n1448\tthe Stage-1 row masks. The warmup ratio was 0.05 and the training random seed\n1449\twas 42. The data split used seed 20260501.\n1450\t\n1451\tC.2 Matched-Data Controls\n1452\t\n1453\tThe no-layout ACE-Step 1.5-FT control uses the same filtered training items,\n1454\taudio codes, backbone LM, optimization precision, and adaptation budget as the\n1455\tfull model, but directly predicts audio tokens without a MusicLayout prefix.\n1456\tThe shuffled-layout-training control retains the MusicLayout-shaped prefix but\n1457\tpairs each audio target with a layout from another item during training. At\n1458\tinference it generates a layout from the prompt and then generates audio, as in\n1459\tthe full generated-layout condition. The shuffled-layout-inference control\n1460\tuses the normally trained MusicLayout model but replaces the item-matched\n1461\treference layout with a valid layout from another item. Thus, the three controls\n1462\tremove layout information, disrupt training-time correspondence, or disrupt\n1463\tinference-time correspondence, respectively.\n1464\t\n1465\tBoth shuffled conditions use seed 20260711. For shuffled-layout training, the\n1466\t24,474 training layouts form a one-to-one permutation of the same training\n1467\titems, with zero fixed points. The 2,719 development items remain unchanged.\n1468\tFor shuffled-layout inference, FreeMIDI and MidiCaps are permuted independently\n1469\twithin their evaluation sets. This yields 3,759 unique target–donor pairs and\n1470\tzero fixed points, preventing cross-dataset donors and self-matches. Donor\n1471\tlayouts are truncated or extended at segment boundaries to end at the target\n1472\titem’s requested duration.\n1473\t\n1474\tAppendix D Baseline Selection and Exact Configurations\n1475\t\n1476\tD.1 Selection Scope\n1477\t\n1478\tWe restricted the baseline comparison to models with publicly released weights\n1479\tand executable inference implementations. We further required compatibility\n1480\twith instrumental prompt-to-music generation and several-minute output, either\n1481\tthrough native variable-duration synthesis or through an established\n1482\tcontinuation mechanism. These criteria allow the reported systems to consume\n1483\tthe same prompts and target durations and to be evaluated using the same audio\n1484\tand structural metrics.\n1485\t\n1486\tAudioLDM 2 [20] and\n1487\tMustango [23] were considered but not included in the\n1488\tfinal comparison. Their released checkpoints and public inference protocols\n1489\tare centered on short clips and do not provide a validated overlapping-context\n1490\tcontinuation procedure for the several-minute setting used here. Concatenating\n1491\tindependently generated diffusion samples would introduce a different and\n1492\tpotentially discontinuous generation procedure. In contrast, the reported\n1493\tdiffusion baseline, Stable Audio 3 Medium [10], supports the required duration\n1494\tnatively.\n1495\t\n1496\tD.2 Executed Configurations\n1497\t\n1498\tTable 9 reports the configurations used in the\n1499\tfinal comparison. Each system targets the item’s manifest duration.\n1500\tACE-Step-family and Stable Audio systems generate that duration natively,\n1501\twithout concatenating independently generated clips. The corresponding model\n1502\tfamilies are described by Gong et al. [13] and Evans et al. [10].\n1503\t\n1504\tSystem\n1505\t\n1506\tCheckpoint/configuration\n1507\t\n1508\tExecuted inference settings\n1509\t\n1510\tMusicGen-Large\n1511\t\n1512\tfacebook/musicgen-large\n1513\t\n1514\t30-s window, 10-s audio context, 20-s retained hop, 51.2 tokens/s, 1,536 first-window and 1,024 continuation tokens, base seed 28602\n1515\t\n1516\tACE-Step 1.5\n1517\t\n1518\tacestep-v15-turbo, acestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B\n1519\t\n1520\t8 inference steps, LM temperature/top- 0.9/0.95, native target duration, base seed 38602\n1521\t\n1522\tACE-Step 1.5 XL-Turbo\n1523\t\n1524\tacestep-v15-xl-turbo, acestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B\n1525\t\n1526\t8 infe\n1527\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8a6a5449-3dfe-4fa1-b2d2-d033dd18ede5", "uuid": "39eb8a4d-aca7-474e-a581-fc6e5b85a9c4", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T09:00:40.986Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/8a6a5449-3dfe-4fa1-b2d2-d033dd18ede5/tool-results/bb3z8cnon.txt", "content": "\n\nMusicLayout: Explicit Structural Planning for Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n\n \n \n\n \n Title:\n \n\n Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n\n Description:\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n Learn more\n ×\n \n\n \n License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n \n \narXiv:2608.09035v1 [cs.SD] 10 Aug 2026\n\nMusicLayout: Explicit Structural Planning\nfor Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n\nShuyu Li1\n\n  \nKejun Zhang1,3\nCorresponding author.\n  \nJiahe Lei4\n\n  \nShulei Ji2,3\n\n  \nZihao Wang2,5\n\n  \nJiaxing Yu1\n\n  \nWanying Wu6\n\n  \nLei Wang7\n\n1College of Artificial Intelligence, Zhejiang University\n2College of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang University\n3Innovation Center of Yangtze River Delta, Zhejiang University\n4The Chinese University of Hong Kong\n5Shandong University\n6Chu Kochen Honors College, Zhejiang University\n7Ant Group\n\n{lsyxary, zhangkejun, shuleiji, yujx, 3240100265}@zju.edu.cn\n1155261729@ee.cuhk.edu.hk, carlwang1212@gmail.com, thirtyking@163.com\n\nAbstract\nText-to-music generation has advanced rapidly, but current systems still rely primarily on global text prompts, leaving the structural organization of generated music implicit and difficult to inspect, control, or revise before audio generation. To address this issue, we introduce MusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for controlling musical structure in text-to-music generation. MusicLayout describes a musical piece as a time-aligned layout of sections, textures, repetitions, variations, and instrument-level arrangements, serving as an interpretable planning layer between textual intent and the generated music. We integrate MusicLayout into a text-to-music framework built on a unified autoregressive formulation, where the model first generates a MusicLayout representation and subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned on this representation within a single sequence. The resulting MusicLayout can be inspected and modified prior to audio generation, providing a mechanism for layout-level structural control. We evaluate MusicLayout through layout-conditioned generation, layout manipulation experiments, and matched-data ablations, providing evidence that explicit layout planning can improve long-range structural organization and support layout-level control.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nRecent text-to-music models have advanced substantially in fidelity,\nsemantic alignment, musicality, and efficiency [19]. They\ntypically generate discrete audio tokens autoregressively or synthesize\ncontinuous and compressed representations with diffusion models\n[1, 4, 20]. Recent\nsystems further support long-form generation and combine language-model (LM)\nreasoning with diffusion-based acoustic synthesis\n[10, 13].\n\nDespite these advances, most systems rely on global text prompts and leave\nmusical planning implicit. A prompt can describe genre, mood,\ninstrumentation, or tempo, but cannot precisely specify how sections are\norganized, materials recur or vary, textures evolve, or instruments enter\nand leave. Existing controllable methods add melody, chords, drums,\ndynamics, symbolic lead sheets, or semantic representations\n[39, 26, 34, 2, 13]. These\ncontrols are often limited to individual attributes, require additional\nmusical inputs, or remain implicit. They do not expose a unified,\ntime-aligned representation for specifying the organization and arrangement\nof an entire piece.\n\nExplicit planning representations improve controllability and\ninterpretability in image generation by exposing high-level organization\nbefore content. PlanGen [14], for example, generates spatial\nlayout tokens before image tokens in one autoregressive sequence. For music,\nthis principle could improve long-range structural organization and\ncontrollability. Transferring it requires representing organization along\ntime rather than space.\n\nFocusing on instrumental music, we introduce\nMusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for planning\nmusical structure in audio language models. It describes\nsection transitions, repetitions and variations, texture changes, and\ninstrument participation along a shared timeline. As illustrated in\nFigure 1, given a text prompt, our model generates a\nMusicLayout representation and subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned\non it within a single sequence. This makes structural\nplanning inspectable before synthesis and supplies audio generation with an\nexplicit description of how the music should unfold. Experiments on\nlayout conditioning and manipulation provide evidence that MusicLayout can\nimprove structural organization and support layout-level control.\n\nFigure 1: Conventional autoregressive LM-based text-to-audio generation (top)\nand our explicit-planning framework (bottom). Our LM generates MusicLayout\ntokens and then discrete audio tokens, labeled audio codes in the figure,\nwithin one sequence. A frozen decoder renders the audio tokens into music.\nThe abbreviated rewrite selects the musical section at 49–55 seconds,\nassigns it the chorus label, and sets its active instrument list to\ndrums. The <roles> tag delimits that list.\n\nOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n\n• \n\nWe propose MusicLayout, an explicit planning\nrepresentation for unified audio language models. It represents music\nas a time-aligned layout of sections, repetitions, variations,\ntextures, and instrument arrangements, providing structured musical\nplans beyond global textual prompts.\n\n• \n\nWe develop a unified autoregressive framework for\nlayout planning and audio generation, where a single model\nfirst produces an explicit MusicLayout plan and then continues to\ngenerate audio tokens conditioned on the preceding layout within a single\nsequence. By making musical planning explicit, this framework\nsupports long-range structural organization and layout-level control.\n\n• \n\nThrough evaluations of layout-conditioned generation,\nlayout manipulation, and matched-data ablations, we provide evidence\nthat MusicLayout can serve as an interpretable planning interface and\nimprove long-range structural organization in text-to-music generation.\n\n2 Related Work\n\n2.1 Text-to-Music Generation\n\nAutoregressive music models predict discrete audio tokens, as exemplified by\nJukebox, MusicLM, and MusicGen\n[6, 1, 4].\nDiffusion-based systems instead synthesize waveforms, spectrograms, or\ncompressed latents through iterative denoising\n[16, 31, 23].\nAudioLDM 2 and MeLoDy combine LM-based semantic modeling with diffusion\nsynthesis [20, 18]. Stable Audio 3 supports\nefficient variable-length generation, while ACE-Step 1.5 uses a hybrid\narchitecture in which an LM performs high-level planning and a Diffusion\nTransformer (DiT) realizes the audio [10, 13, 28]. Although\nsome systems incorporate high-level planning, temporal organization and\narrangement remain implicit or coarsely represented.\n\n2.2 Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n\nPrior systems supplement global prompts with melody references, rhythm,\ndynamics, chords, drums, or other symbolic and audio conditions\n[1, 4, 39, 26, 34].\nSeed-Music uses a symbolic lead-sheet pipeline [2]. ACE-Step 1.5 uses metadata and\nsong-blueprint planning, but its blueprint remains implicit in the learned\naudio-token representations and is not exposed as an interpretable structural plan\n[13]. Other methods use individual controls,\nadditional inputs, task-specific modules, or coarse metadata. MusicLayout\ninstead exposes sections, textures, repetitions, variations, and instrument\narrangements in an inspectable and adjustable time-aligned plan.\n\n3 Methodology\n\nWe build our model upon ACE-Step 1.5, extending its LM to generate a\nMusicLayout before producing layout-conditioned audio tokens. The original\naudio tokenizer and DiT remain frozen, with the latter rendering the tokens\ninto a waveform.\n\nFigure 2: A MusicLayout example aligned to a shared timeline. Rows show sections,\nmaterial families, textures, variations, and instrument arrangements. Family\ncolors identify recurring material, and instrument entries give register,\ndensity, and energy. Blanks denote inactivity. For readability, rhythm, light, and\nstrong abbreviate rhythm_driven, light_var, and\nstrong_var, respectively.\n\n3.1 MusicLayout Representation\n\nMusicLayout externalizes temporal musical organization as a structured,\nhuman-readable sequence that bridges high-level structural intent and\naudio-token generation. It follows three design principles: temporal\nalignment, hierarchical organization, and controllability.\n\nA MusicLayout combines piece-level structural relationships among musical\nmaterials with time-aligned segments, each corresponding to one musical\nsection.\nAt the piece level, MusicLayout organizes recurring musical materials\nand their relationships, including repetitions and variations across\nsections. At the segment level, each segment is associated with a\ntime-aligned span and describes its section identity, texture evolution,\nand instrument participation.\nIts functional section label is selected from intro, verse,\nprechorus, chorus, bridge, breakdown,\noutro, transition, hook, solo, and\nbuild.\nFigure 2 illustrates these attributes along a\nshared timeline.\nMusicLayout is serialized as discrete tokens, allowing it to be directly\nmodeled by an autoregressive LM.\n\nA family is a piece-local group of segments that share similar musical\nmaterial. Labels such as fam_0 and fam_1 identify families\nonly within the current piece. The same label may denote different musical\nmaterial in another piece. Each segment is marked\nas unique, same, light_var, or\nstrong_var. These values indicate, respectively, that the segment\nhas no repeated family, closely repeats its family pattern, or departs from\nthat pattern to a smaller or larger degree. Thus, the family colors and the\nvariation row in Figure 2 jointly describe which\nmaterial returns and how much it changes.\n\nCategory\n\nMeaning\n\nlayered\n\nCoexisting lead and supporting layers\n\nrhythm_driven\n\nRhythm-section-centered arrangement\n\nmelodic_front\n\nForegrounded melodic material\n\nharmonic_bed\n\nSustained harmonic support\n\npercussive\n\nPercussion-centered material\n\nbuild_up\n\nAccumulating or intensifying layers\n\nsparse_pulse\n\nSparse intermittent activity\n\nlead_front\n\nForegrounded lead material\n\ncontrast\n\nDeliberately contrasting texture\n\nother\n\nCases outside the listed categories\n\nTable 1: Texture categories in MusicLayout.\n\nThe texture field gives a coarse description of how a musical segment is\norganized. Table 1 defines its vocabulary. Each\nsegment contains a <roles> block listing its active instruments.\nFigures 1 and 2 use the\nform instrument:register:density:energy,\nfor example drums:low:sparse:med.\nThe instrument field uses a predefined vocabulary of 25 categories. Each\ninstrument is followed by register\n(low/mid/high), density\n(sparse/med/dense), and energy\n(low/med/high), encoding pitch range, activity, and\nintensity, respectively.\nFigure 1 abbreviates the edited field as drums.\n\nWe derived MusicLayout annotations from symbolic representations by extracting\ntemporal sections, repetitions, variations, textures, and instrument\narrangements. We rendered the symbolic music into audio and aligned each layout\nwith its audio and text prompt to form prompt–layout–audio training triples.\n\n3.2 Unified Layout Planning and Audio Generation\n\nModeling.\nGiven conditioning context , the LM serializes MusicLayout sequence \nbefore audio-token sequence within a single autoregressive sequence:\n\n(1)\n\nwhere <layout> and </layout> delimit the planning region.\nThis ordering makes layout generation precede and condition audio-token\ngeneration. The corresponding joint distribution factorizes as\n\n(2)\n\nHere, describes layout planning from the conditioning context,\nwhile describes audio-token generation given the context and\nlayout.\n\nTraining.\nWe train the LM on two complementary next-token tasks. Layout planning trains\nthe model to generate from . Layout-conditioned audio generation trains\nit to predict from and a ground-truth (GT) MusicLayout:\n\n(3)\n\nWe initially optimize layout planning to learn the MusicLayout schema and the\nschema-specific special tokens used to mark its fields and boundaries. We then\nalternate the tasks, applying the loss only to the\ncorresponding layout or audio target span. Only the LM is updated. The\npretrained audio tokenizer and DiT synthesis components remain frozen.\n\nInference and control.\nIn automatic inference, the LM generates a MusicLayout from the conditioning\ncontext and then continues with audio tokens. Alternatively, the human-readable\nlayout can be inspected and adjusted before audio generation to change temporal\norganization or instrument arrangement. The revised layout and original\ncontext form the autoregressive prefix for audio-token generation, providing a\npre-synthesis interface for layout-level control.\n\n4 Experiments\n\nWe evaluate generation, structural control, layout manipulation, ablations,\nand subjective quality against representative text-to-music systems.\nFor our framework, we consider two layout conditions. In the reference-layout\ncondition, the model receives a MusicLayout derived from the target music. This\ncondition jointly tests whether MusicLayout captures the target’s musical\norganization and whether the model can use it to control the structure of the\ngenerated audio. In the generated-layout condition, the model predicts a layout\nfrom the text prompt before generating audio, evaluating the complete\nend-to-end text-to-music process.\n\n4.1 Experimental Setup\n\nDatasets.\n\nWe used three datasets with complementary roles. FreeMIDI [12]\nprovided the training data and a disjoint in-domain evaluation set.\nMidiCaps [24] was used to evaluate generalization to\nout-of-domain MIDI, while MuChin [38] provided an out-of-domain\nevaluation on real audio. Their details are summarized in\nTable 2.\n\nDataset\nAudio source\nTrain\nEval.\n\nFreeMIDI\nMIDI-synth.\n24,474\n2,719\n\nMidiCaps\nMIDI-synth.\n–\n1,040\n\nMuChin\nReal, separated accomp.\n–\n1,000\n\nTable 2: Datasets used in our experiments.\n\nFreeMIDI.\nWe collected a subset of FreeMIDI and retained only pieces longer than 15 seconds.\nFor each remaining MIDI file, we extracted a MusicLayout and synthesized its\naudio, forming a layout–audio pair. We then\nprovided the audio to MOSS-Music [27], an audio-understanding language model, to produce\nthe generation prompt, yielding aligned prompt–layout–audio triples.\n\nMidiCaps.\nMidiCaps already provides natural-language captions. We selected a subset with\na balanced distribution of style labels and removed MIDI files that overlap\nwith FreeMIDI. For each remaining piece, we extracted a layout and synthesized\naudio from the MIDI, directly pairing both with its caption to form evaluation\ntriples.\n\nMuChin.\nMuChin contains vocal music with Chinese captions but no MIDI, and thus cannot\nsupport the reference-layout condition. We used DeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] to translate the captions into\nEnglish and remove vocal-related descriptions. We further separated the\naccompaniment from the vocals using a community-trained BS-RoFormer\nmodel [22] and\nused only the separated accompaniment for metric computation. Generation\nconditions that require an externally supplied layout—the\nreference-layout and shuffled-layout-inference conditions—are not reported\non MuChin.\n\nCompared systems.\n\nWe compared against MusicGen [4], ACE-Step 1.5 and\nACE-Step XL-Turbo [13], and Stable Audio 3 Medium\n[10]. We evaluated our model under either the\ngenerated-layout or the reference-layout condition. To control for the effect of additional\ntraining data, we also finetuned ACE-Step 1.5 on the same data using the same LM\nadaptation setup as our model. We denote this matched-data no-layout control as\nACE-Step 1.5-FT. It directly predicts audio from the conditioning context \nwithout MusicLayout tokens, serving as both the primary fair comparison for explicit layout planning and\nthe first step of the progressive ablation. Systems shared evaluation items,\nprompts, and seeds when supported, and baselines used officially recommended\ndecoding settings. Systems without a layout representation\ntargeted each item’s reference duration.\nFor long-form MusicGen generation, we followed its\nofficial continuation mechanism.\nFor our model, the generation\nduration is instead determined by the end time of the conditioning layout. In\nthe generated-layout condition, this duration is therefore predicted by the\nmodel rather than specified externally, whereas the reference-layout\ncondition matches the reference duration because its layout is derived from\nthe target music.\n\nEvaluation protocol.\n\nWe report Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD) [30] for acoustic\ndistribution similarity, PaSST-KL [17] for sound-event\nagreement, SSIM [37] for local time–frequency similarity,\nand CLAPScore [7] for text–audio alignment.\n\nFor long-range structure, SCM Energy Distance\n[5, 33] compares corpus-level structural\ncomplexity distributions, while and acoustic-boundary\nagreement [36] measure fine- and coarse-grained\ntransition alignment within each piece.\nTo examine how layout manipulation changes long-range organization, we also\nvisualize recurrence-based self-similarity matrices (SSMs), following the\nstructural visualization used in Stable Audio 2 and its underlying music\nstructure analysis method [9, 32].\n\nImplementation details.\n\nWe finetuned ACE-Step 1.5’s 1.7B-parameter LM using six A800 GPUs, a\n4,096-token context, and an effective batch size of 48. We selected the\ncheckpoint with the lowest development layout-to-audio loss. Layout and audio\ndecoding used\ntemperature/top- values of and .\n\n4.2 Results\n\nOverall Generation Performance\n\nDataset\nGroup\nSystem\n\nFAD\n\nKL\n\nSSIM\n\nCLAP\n\nSCM\n\nFreeMIDI\nBaselines\nACE-Step 1.5\n2.874\n0.789\n0.159\n0.284\n0.117\n0.637\n0.831\n\nACE-Step XL-Turbo\n2.584\n0.734\n0.181\n0.300\n0.063\n0.619\n0.811\n\nMusicGen-Large\n3.706\n0.834\n0.189\n0.280\n0.375\n0.558\n0.757\n\nStable Audio 3 Medium\n3.014\n0.838\n0.213\n0.378\n0.181\n0.616\n0.809\n\nOur model\nReference layout\n2.610\n0.740\n0.183\n0.238\n0.054\n0.645\n0.838\n\nGenerated layout\n2.495\n0.719\n0.153\n0.241\n0.206\n0.643\n0.834\n\nMatched-data\nACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout)\n3.345\n0.956\n0.154\n0.219\n0.380\n0.577\n0.739\n\ncontrols\nShuffled-layout training\n2.756\n0.721\n0.153\n0.216\n0.100\n0.607\n0.810\n\nShuffled-layout inference\n2.636\n0.779\n0.176\n0.230\n0.038\n0.642\n0.831\n\nMidiCaps\nBaselines\nACE-Step 1.5\n2.526\n0.797\n0.152\n0.290\n0.110\n0.632\n0.828\n\nACE-Step XL-Turbo\n3.036\n0.756\n0.174\n0.313\n0.100\n0.606\n0.801\n\nMusicGen-Large\n2.488\n0.825\n0.180\n0.258\n0.379\n0.545\n0.746\n\nStable Audio 3 Medium\n2.123\n0.726\n0.229\n0.352\n0.315\n0.616\n0.812\n\nOur model\nReference layout\n2.149\n0.712\n0.169\n0.305\n0.087\n0.635\n0.836\n\nGenerated layout\n2.303\n0.699\n0.140\n0.287\n0.804\n0.630\n0.830\n\nMatched-data\nACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout)\n2.740\n0.877\n0.153\n0.234\n0.351\n0.560\n0.736\n\ncontrols\nShuffled-layout training\n2.772\n0.734\n0.144\n0.267\n0.136\n0.599\n0.807\n\nShuffled-layout inference\n2.192\n0.740\n0.165\n0.293\n0.117\n0.635\n0.834\n\nMuChin\nBaselines\nACE-Step 1.5\n2.480\n0.685\n0.129\n0.289\n0.186\n0.588\n0.797\n\nACE-Step XL-Turbo\n1.994\n0.617\n0.147\n0.317\n0.611\n0.566\n0.779\n\nMusicGen-Large\n3.279\n0.717\n0.156\n0.241\n0.215\n0.517\n0.756\n\nStable Audio 3 Medium\n2.007\n0.662\n0.177\n0.365\n0.132\n0.573\n0.794\n\nOur model\nGenerated layout\n3.456\n0.671\n0.116\n0.285\n0.655\n0.594\n0.809\n\nMatched-data\nACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout)\n3.124\n0.844\n0.123\n0.222\n1.458\n0.535\n0.726\n\ncontrols\nShuffled-layout training\n3.714\n0.670\n0.120\n0.286\n0.626\n0.600\n0.820\n\nTable 3: Objective results. CLAP, KL, and SCM abbreviate CLAPScore, PaSST-KL,\nand SCM Energy Distance. Bold and underline mark the first- and second-ranked\nnumerical values per dataset, with rounded ties bold. The three matched-data\ncontrols remove MusicLayout or mismatch it in training or inference.\n\nTable 3 summarizes the objective results. No single\nsystem dominates all aspects of generation.\nOur model with generated layouts records the lowest FAD and PaSST-KL on\nFreeMIDI. On MidiCaps, it also records the lowest PaSST-KL, suggesting that\nlayout-conditioned generation can remain close to the reference acoustic-event\ndistribution despite out-of-domain MIDI captions. Its lower CLAPScore relative\nto Stable Audio and ACE-Step XL-Turbo, however, shows that explicit planning\nmay not uniformly improve global text–audio correspondence.\n\nThe matched-data no-layout control provides a more direct test of the\ncontribution of explicit layout planning. It is weaker than the original\nACE-Step 1.5 on nearly all metrics. This degradation\nlikely reflects the training-data difference: the original model was trained\non high-quality real recordings, whereas our finetuning data consists of\nMIDI-synthesized audio with lower audio quality and timbral fidelity, as well\nas a different acoustic distribution. Since our model and this control share\nthe same finetuning data and LM adaptation setup, their comparison isolates\nthe effect of explicit layout planning. Relative to this control, the\nreference-layout condition has more favorable values on all seven metrics for\nFreeMIDI and MidiCaps. The generated-layout condition does so on six metrics\nfor FreeMIDI and five for MidiCaps and MuChin.\nThese\nresults show that explicit layout planning substantially improves generation under matched-data\nconditions and can partly offset the limitations of weaker training audio.\n\nOverall, our model is competitive on the two MIDI-derived datasets,\nindicating that explicit layout planning contributes to overall music\ngeneration performance. Its FAD, PaSST-KL, and SSIM results on MuChin are\nless favorable, however. This difference likely arises from an acoustic-domain\nshift: our model is trained on MIDI-synthesized instrumental audio, whereas\nMuChin consists of accompaniments separated from real recordings.\n\nStructural Control\n\nThe reference-layout condition records the highest boundary scores shown for\nFreeMIDI, the highest MidiCaps , and a tie for the highest MidiCaps\n.\nBecause the reference layouts are derived from the target music, these results\nprovide evidence that MusicLayout captures target organization and that\nconditioning on it can guide corresponding structure in the generated audio.\n\nWith model-generated layouts, our model also shows strong structural\norganization without access to the target-derived layout used in the\nreference-layout condition. On FreeMIDI, both boundary scores are numerically\nabove all baselines. On MidiCaps, is numerically above all baselines,\nwhile remains competitive. On MuChin, both boundary scores are\nnumerically above all baselines, showing that this advantage persists under an\nacoustic-domain shift from MIDI-synthesized training data to accompaniments\nseparated from real recordings. These results\nindicate that explicit intermediate planning improves the organization of\nlong-range musical structure in end-to-end generation.\n\nThe comparison with the matched-data no-layout control further isolates this\nstructural benefit. Both layout conditions have higher boundary scores than\nthe no-layout control wherever available, and the generated-layout condition\nretains this advantage across all three datasets.\nThus, under the same MIDI-synthesized training data, explicit layout planning\nimproves boundary agreement in both controlled and automatic generation.\n\nSCM Energy Distance measures agreement between dataset-level structural\ncomplexity distributions rather than song-level layout correctness. On the\ntwo MIDI-derived evaluation sets, our model in the reference-layout condition\nrecords the lowest observed SCM Energy Distance among the non-ablation systems\n, indicating that the specified layouts are realized with corpus-level\nstructural complexity close to the references.\nOn MuChin,\nthe SCM Energy\nDistance in the generated-layout condition is comparatively high. This weaker agreement is consistent with the\ntraining–evaluation\nshift from MIDI-synthesized instrumental music to accompaniments separated\nfrom real vocal recordings, which have substantially different\nstructural-complexity distributions. However, with training data controlled,\nthe generated-layout condition has lower SCM than the no-layout condition.\nThis comparison suggests a structural benefit from layout planning.\n\nFigure 3: Structural effects of layout manipulation for two FreeMIDI and two\nMidiCaps examples. Each row presents, from left to right, the target GT under\nPrompt A and Layout A, generation under the same prompt and reference layout,\nthe layout-donor GT under Prompt B and Layout B,\nand generation from Prompt A paired with Layout B. For clear visualization,\nSSM intensities are normalized independently within each panel. Darker regions\nwithin a panel indicate stronger recurrence. Audio signals in each row are\ncropped to their common minimum duration.\n\nStructural Effects of Layout Manipulation\n\nFigure 3 presents four separate\nlayout-manipulation cases, with two drawn from FreeMIDI and two from MidiCaps.\nThese cases examine how layout manipulation changes the generated structure\nwhile the text prompt is held fixed. In each\nrow, item A serves as the target and provides Prompt A and its reference Layout\nA. A different item B supplies Layout B, which we refer to as the donor layout.\nThe SSM for item B is included to visualize the recurrence structure associated\nwith Layout B. We refer to generation from Prompt A and Layout A as the matched\ngeneration, and generation from the same prompt and Layout B as the manipulated\ngeneration. In these examples, the matched generation\nshows recurrence patterns similar to the target reference, while the\nmanipulated generation loses some target-aligned patterns and exhibits patterns\ncloser to the donor reference. Because the manipulated generation combines the\ntarget prompt with the donor layout, it can retain structural characteristics\nassociated with the target while adopting aspects of the donor’s organization.\nTogether, these cases illustrate that MusicLayout can be manipulated before\naudio generation to control the structure of the generated music.\n\nFine-Grained Regional Controls\n\nWe further tested three direct edits to a single 240-second MusicLayout,\nholding the text prompt and sampling seeds fixed so that each controlled\ncondition differed from the original only in the declared MusicLayout fields.\nFigure 4 compares the original and\ncontrolled audio over the affected interval.\n\nFirst, in the 0–11-second introduction, we replaced sparse, high-energy\nstrings and synthesizer strings with dense, high-energy drums and sparse,\nhigh-energy bass. This edit was expected to add stronger low-frequency rhythmic\ncontent and broadband percussive transients. The controlled spectrogram shows\nboth effects: energy below 250 Hz becomes dominant and repeated vertical\ntransients appear across the band.\n\nSecond, over 142–196 seconds, we changed every active role to sparse density\nand medium energy while leaving the segment boundaries, labels, families, and\ninstrument identities unchanged. The expected result was a less dense and\nless intense realization of the same region. Relative to the original, the\ncontrolled excerpt has visibly reduced broadband activity and its RMS level\ndecreases by 15% (1.4 dB).\n\nThird, we preserved the complete 0–91-second prefix and rewrote the remaining\nform from a largely repeated chorus sequence into a breakdown, bridge, build,\nsolo, chorus, and outro, together with their associated roles and recurrence\nfamilies. The expected result was a new long-range progression after the\npreserved prefix. The controlled spectrogram exhibits the intended succession\nof contrasting regimes, including the reduced texture of the breakdown and\nbridge, the subsequent build, and the denser solo and chorus.\nThus, all three edits produce changes consistent with their specified local or\nregional controls.\n\nFigure 4: Paired log-mel spectrograms for three fine-grained MusicLayout\ncontrols: (a) role replacement over 0–11 seconds, (b) reduced density and\nenergy over 142–196 seconds, and (c) a form rewrite over 91–240 seconds.\nLight-blue boxes use matched time–frequency coordinates: A1/A2 mark\nlow-frequency and broadband changes, B marks reduced broadband activity, and\nC1–C3 mark successive stages of the form rewrite. Each pair shares its 0-dB\nreference and 80-dB range. Intensities are comparable within a row. Time axes\nretain full-song coordinates.\n\nAblation Study\n\nThe three matched-data controls in Table 3 form a\nprogressive ablation of MusicLayout. First, the finetuned ACE-Step 1.5 control\nremoves MusicLayout entirely. Second, shuffled-layout training introduces\nMusicLayout but pairs each training item with a layout from another item, so\nthe model learns from mismatched layout–audio pairs. At inference, this model\ngenerates its own layout from the prompt before audio generation, following the\nsame end-to-end procedure as the full generated-layout condition. Third, shuffled-layout\ninference restores correctly paired layouts during training but replaces the\nconditioning layout with one from another item at inference.\n\nShuffled-layout training has favorable values on five of seven metrics relative to the no-layout control for\neach of the three datasets. This pattern suggests that\nlayout-shaped supervision may retain\na generic structural benefit even when item-level information is incorrect.\nOn the two MIDI-synthesized datasets, our full model with generated layouts\nhas favorable values on five of seven metrics relative to shuffled-layout training for both\nFreeMIDI and MidiCaps. Because both conditions generate layouts at inference,\nthis pattern suggests that correct layout–audio correspondence during training\nprovides information beyond the presence of layout-shaped tokens and can\nimprove long-form music generation when the evaluation domain matches the type\nof audio used for training. On MuChin, the two conditions obtain broadly similar\nresults. This may result from the acoustic-domain shift discussed above, since our\nmodel was trained on MIDI-synthesized audio rather than accompaniments from\nreal recordings.\n\nHolding correctly paired training fixed, the reference-layout condition has favorable\nFAD, KL, SSIM, CLAPScore, and values relative to shuffled-layout inference on both MIDI-derived\ndatasets. This indicates that item-matched layouts provide a more informative\ninference-time conditioning signal than a plausible but unrelated layout.\n\nA shuffled layout remains musically plausible because it comes from a real\npiece and retains valid sections, timing, repetitions, and arrangements.\nShuffling can therefore preserve the corpus-level layout distribution while\nbreaking item-level correspondence, so low SCM alone does not demonstrate\ncorrect layout control.\n\n4.3 Subjective Evaluation\n\nAll ()\n\nExp. ()\n\nSystem\nF\nM\nT\nF\nM\nT\n\nGT\n3.200\n3.490\n3.408\n3.000\n3.361\n3.300\n\nACE 1.5\n3.283\n3.329\n3.225\n3.031\n3.342\n3.108\n\nACE-Step 1.5-FT\n2.931\n2.632\n2.702\n2.631\n2.386\n2.661\n\nOurs-Ref\n3.064\n3.012\n3.093\n2.989\n2.917\n3.183\n\nOurs-Gen\n2.914\n2.586\n2.869\n2.733\n2.783\n2.839\n\nTable 4: Subjective ratings on FreeMIDI and MidiCaps (0–5). F/M/T\ndenote fidelity, musical impression, and text consistency. Exp. denotes\nexperienced listeners, and FT denotes matched-data finetuning.\n\nSystem\nScore\n\nACE-Step 1.5\n2.319\n\nACE-Step 1.5-FT\n1.444\n\nOurs (reference layout)\n2.556\n\nOurs (generated layout)\n2.044\n\nTable 5: GT-reference structural similarity rated by\nmusic-experienced participants on the same MIDI-derived items (0–5).\n\nTo enable a direct comparison with the reference-layout condition, we excluded\nMuChin because it does not provide reference layouts. We sampled 10 items each\nfrom FreeMIDI and MidiCaps and assigned them repeatedly to 27 participants,\nincluding 15 with prior music experience. Participants rated audio fidelity,\nmusical impression, and text consistency on a 0–5 scale, with one decimal\nplace allowed. Each evaluation compared all five systems,\nyielding 59 ratings per system, including 36 ratings from experienced\nlisteners.\n\nTable 4 shows that comparisons with the matched-data\nno-layout control vary with layout condition and listener experience. Across\nall participants, the reference-layout condition has higher observed scores on\nall three dimensions. Generated-layout scores are broadly comparable,\nwith similar fidelity and musical impression but higher text consistency.\nWithin the experienced subset, both layout conditions score numerically higher\nthan the control across all dimensions. Notably, the reference-layout condition\nalso has higher observed text consistency than the original ACE-Step 1.5 among\nexperienced listeners (3.183 vs. 3.108), suggesting that an explicit\nMusicLayout can provide an additional control signal for steering music\ngeneration. This pattern may arise because\nmusic-experienced listeners are more attentive to structural organization when\nevaluating generated music. ACE-Step 1.5 has the highest observed fidelity\nscore across the five systems (3.283), above GT (3.200). As discussed above,\nthis difference is consistent with the acoustic-quality gap between the\nMIDI-synthesized data in our experiments and the real recordings used to train\nACE-Step 1.5. It\nunderscores the need for matched-data controls to\nisolate layout planning from training-data differences.\n\nUsing the same dataset scope, we conducted a follow-up on a subset of the\npreviously sampled items. A subset of the experienced participants was shown\nthe GT reference for each item and asked to rate the structural\nsimilarity of the remaining outputs to the references. The reference-layout\ncondition received a higher observed structural\nsimilarity than ACE-Step 1.5, providing subjective evidence that MusicLayout\ncaptures reference organization and carries it into generated audio. The\ngenerated-layout condition was slightly weaker because coarse dataset prompts\ndo not uniquely specify the GT layout. Once predicted, the layout steers\ngeneration toward its own plausible structure. The objective structural\nmetrics above suggest that these outputs can remain structurally organized even\nwhen they do not reproduce the particular GT structure.\n\n5 Conclusion\n\nWe presented MusicLayout, an explicit, time-aligned representation of section\norganization, development, and instrument arrangement for a unified\nautoregressive audio LM. Generated before audio tokens, it makes structural\nplanning inspectable and adjustable. The same LM plans the layout and predicts\naudio tokens conditioned on it while the synthesis components remain frozen.\nOur results suggest that MusicLayout provides an interpretable interface that\nsupports long-range structural organization and layout-level control.\n\nDespite these benefits, MusicLayout adjusts structural plans only before\nsynthesis and cannot edit existing audio or regenerate selected regions.\nOur reliance on MIDI-synthesized training audio may also limit audio fidelity.\nFuture work will pursue finer control and higher-quality, structurally aligned\naudio data.\n\nAcknowledgments\n\nThis work was supported by Ant Group.\n\nAppendix A MusicLayout Representation and Annotation\n\nA.1 Serialization Grammar\n\nMusicLayout is serialized as a discrete text sequence with one optional\npiece-level family block and one required segment block. A family records a\npiece-local identifier, its member segments, its cardinality, and whether the\nmembers are adjacent. Each segment records an integer-second time span, a\nfunctional section label, a texture, an arrangement-change label, its family\nmembership and family role, the degree of variation from the family prototype,\na repetition flag, and a list of active instrument tuples. The grammar used in\nall reported experiments is summarized below:\n\n<layout>\n<families>\n<fam> id=fam_K members=sI,sJ count=N adj=true|false </fam>\n</families>\n<segments>\n<seg> id=sI time=A-B label=L texture=T change=C\n fam=fam_K fam_role=R variation=V repeat=true|false\n<roles> instrument:register:density:energy | ... </roles>\n</seg>\n</segments>\n</layout>\n\nFamily identifiers are local to a piece rather than global semantic labels.\nFor example, fam_0 denotes one recurring material within the current\npiece and may refer to unrelated material in another piece. The extraction\npipeline retains singleton material as an explicitly declared one-member\nfamily with count=1 and adj=true. Its segment uses\nvariation=unique and repeat=false. For a recurring family,\nthe first chronological member serves as its variation prototype and is marked\nsame. Later members are marked same, light_var, or\nstrong_var according to their distance from that prototype.\n\nA.2 Closed Vocabularies\n\nTable 6 gives the structural vocabularies.\nThe change field describes the segment’s arrangement-level relation\nto its predecessor, whereas fam_role describes its role in the\npiece-level recurrence organization. These fields are distinct from the\nfunctional section label.\n\nField\n\nAllowed values\n\nSection label\n\nintro, verse, prechorus,\nchorus, bridge, breakdown, outro,\ntransition, hook, solo, build\n\nTexture\n\nlayered, rhythm_driven,\nmelodic_front, harmonic_bed, percussive,\nbuild_up, sparse_pulse, lead_front,\ncontrast, other\n\nChange\n\nentry, continuation, lift,\ndrop, contrast, outro\n\nFamily role\n\nintro_anchor, primary_repeat,\nsecondary_repeat, adjacent_variant,\noutro_anchor, unique\n\nVariation\n\nunique, same, light_var,\nstrong_var\n\nRegister\n\nlow, mid, high\n\nDensity\n\nsparse, med, dense\n\nEnergy\n\nlow, med, high\n\nTable 6: Closed structural and arrangement vocabularies in MusicLayout.\n\nThe instrument field uses 25 compact categories:\nacoustic_piano, electric_piano,\nplucked_keyboard, mallet_bell, organ,\naccordion_harmonica, acoustic_guitar,\nelectric_guitar, bass, strings,\norchestral, synth_strings, choir_voice,\nbrass, sax, woodwind,\nsynth_lead, synth_pad, synth_fx,\nworld_plucked, percussion,\nreverse_cymbal, sound_fx, drums, and\nother. General MIDI program numbers are mapped deterministically to\nthese categories. Instrument entries retain the segment-local register,\ndensity, and energy attributes. These attributes are defined at the instrument\ntuple level rather than duplicated as separate segment-level fields.\n\nA.3 Variation Strength\n\nFor each recurring family, we compare every later segment with the first family\nmember. Let be the Jaccard distance between active-role sets, and let\n, , and be normalized differences in energy, note density, and\nactive-role count. The base distance is\n\n(4)\n\nThe implementation adds small deterministic adjustments when coarse energy,\ndensity, texture, arrangement-change, repeat-neighbor, or source-relation\nevidence differs from the family prototype. We serialize scores at most \nas same, scores in as light_var, and larger\nscores as strong_var. This field\ntherefore records an interpretable, rule-derived degree of variation rather\nthan a human rating.\n\nA.4 Validation\n\nBefore a generated layout can condition audio decoding, the parser checks the\nouter layout and segment wrappers, unique segment identifiers, nondecreasing\ninteger-second start times, positive spans, valid closed-vocabulary values,\nfour-field instrument tuples, family-member counts, and references from\nsegments to declared families. It also rejects legacy bar spans, legacy role\ntags, segment-level energy/density fields, internal MIDI program slugs, and\naudio-code tokens inside the layout. A failed layout is not silently repaired\nor rewritten.\n\nA.5 Annotation Pipeline\n\nThe annotation pipeline begins from aligned MIDI and rendered WAV files. It\nextracts bar-level activity and instrument statistics, combines change and\nrepetition evidence into time-aligned segments, groups structurally related\nsegments into piece-local material families, and derives the section, texture,\nchange, family, variation, and instrument attributes serialized above. MIDI\nprograms are used only as annotation evidence. The learning target remains\naudio-token generation. Segment spans are converted to audio time and rounded\nto integer seconds, with a minimum duration of one second after rounding.\n\nThe extraction procedure is deterministic once its feature and boundary\nsettings are fixed. Algorithm 1 summarizes its\nexecution order.\nThe symbolic representation supplies the structural evidence. The paired\nwaveform is used to place the resulting spans on the rendered-audio timeline.\n\nAlgorithm 1 MusicLayout extraction from aligned symbolic music and audio\n\n1:MIDI performance , aligned waveform duration , and fixed extraction settings \n\n2:Valid serialized layout , or rejection symbol \n\n3:function ExtractMusicLayout()\n\n4:   notes, programs, MIDI duration, bars, beats\n\n5:  for do\n\n6:    density, energy, pitch, and six broad-role activities\n\n7:  end for\n\n8:  \n\n9:  \n\n10:  \n\n11:  \n\n12:  \n\n13:   adjacent-repeat and local-change tests\n\n14:  \n\n15:  \n\n16:  \n\n17:  \n\n18:  for do\n\n19:   \n\n20:   \n\n21:  end for\n\n22:  \n\n23:  for do chronological greedy centroid assignment\n\n24:   \n\n25:   if or then\n\n26:     \n\n27:   else\n\n28:     \n\n29:   end if\n\n30:  end for\n\n31:  for do\n\n32:    relation evidence, not family assignment\n\n33:  end for\n\n34:  \n\n35:  for do\n\n36:   \n\n37:   for do\n\n38:     \n\n39:   end for\n\n40:  end for\n\n41:  \n\n42:  for do\n\n43:   \n\n44:  end for\n\n45:  \n\n46:  if then\n\n47:   return \n\n48:  end if\n\n49:  return \n\n50:end function\n\nAppendix B Dataset Construction and Provenance\n\nB.1 FreeMIDI\n\nFreeMIDI [12] supplies the training corpus and an in-domain evaluation set. We\nretained pieces longer than 15 seconds, extracted one MusicLayout from each\nretained MIDI file, and synthesized the aligned audio at 44.1 kHz using the\nFluidSynth [11] interface provided by\nPrettyMIDI [29], with\nMuseScore_General.sf2 [25]. Of 27,237\nextracted items, 27,229 received a full-song caption from\nMOSS-Music-8B-Instruct [27]. Caption decoding was greedy. The prompt requested one\nconcise English paragraph grounded only in the audio, covering style, audible\ninstrumentation and roles, rhythm, texture, energy, musical development, and\nmood. It explicitly prohibited the use of filenames, MIDI metadata, external\nlabels, bullet lists, and unsupported claims.\n\nRequiring at least 15 seconds of audio and a nonempty supervised audio-code\nspan retained 27,198 items. Five additional items were removed because their\ncomplete planning sequences exceeded the 4,096-token context. The resulting\n27,193 items were split deterministically into 24,474 training and 2,719\ndevelopment items with seed 20260501. The development split is also the\nin-domain evaluation set.\n\nB.2 MidiCaps\n\nMidiCaps [24] is an out-of-domain MIDI evaluation\nset. We first sampled 1,100 items\nwith seed 42 after requiring a duration of at least 120 seconds, at least two\ninstruments, and a nonempty caption. Genre-quota sampling followed by\ninstrument-diverse greedy selection covered 40 source genre labels. Because\nthe labels are multi-valued, their frequencies need not sum to the number of\nitems. The most frequent labels in the final set are electronic (581), pop\n(463), rock (208), classical (108), soundtrack (102), ambient (81), and jazz\n(59). Exact SHA-256 comparison of the MIDI files against the complete FreeMIDI\ncorpus removed 60 overlaps, leaving 1,040 unique items. The source location and\nMIDI SHA-256 jointly define item identity. We then extracted MusicLayouts and\nsynthesized aligned audio using the same renderer as for FreeMIDI. Original\nMidiCaps captions were retained as prompts, and no MidiCaps item was used for\ntraining.\n\nB.3 MuChin\n\nMuChin [38] provides an out-of-domain real-audio evaluation. Because it does not\nprovide MIDI, it cannot supply a reference MusicLayout. We constructed a fixed\n1,000-item manifest in the source metadata order. For each item,\nDeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] received the original full-song\ndescription as context and a\nset of non-vocal tags covering instrumentation, arrangement, style, tempo,\nrhythm, and mood. The instruction required one concise English paragraph using\nonly these safe musical attributes and explicitly prohibited any mention or\nimplication of singers, vocals, lyrics, rap, choir, humming, or spoken dialogue.\nDecoding used temperature 0.2 and top- 0.9. We appended the same explicit\ninstrumental-only control phrase to every final prompt.\n\nWe separated accompaniment from vocals with the Python toolkit\naudio-separator v0.44.3 [3] and the\nBS-RoFormer [22] Viperx-1297 checkpoint [35],\ndistributed as\nmodel_bs_roformer_ep_317_sdr_12.9755.ckpt.\nWe used the resulting accompaniment as the metric reference.\nReference-layout and inference-time shuffled-reference-layout conditions are\nconsequently not applicable to MuChin.\n\nDataset\nTrain\nEvaluation\nRef. layout\n\nFreeMIDI\n24,474\n2,719\nYes\n\nMidiCaps\n0\n1,040\nYes\n\nMuChin\n0\n1,000\nNo\n\nTable 7: Dataset roles and final item counts.\n\nAppendix C Model Adaptation and Training\n\nC.1 Training Sequences and Loss Masks\n\nThe adapted model uses the 1.7B-parameter LM from\nACE-Step 1.5 [13]. Layout planning\nand audio-token prediction share the same LM but use separate supervised target\nspans. In the planning task, the prompt and chat context are inputs and the\nMusicLayout tokens are targets. In the layout-to-audio task, the prompt and\nground-truth MusicLayout form the input prefix and only the following audio-code\ntokens are targets. Chain-of-thought metadata is retained in the serialized\nprefix but receives zero loss weight. The pretrained 5-Hz audio tokenizer and\nDiT renderer [28] remain frozen in all stages.\n\nStage 1 trains only the newly introduced vocabulary rows in the input\nembedding and LM head. Old vocabulary rows are masked, and no transformer layer\nor final normalization parameter is updated. Stage 2 updates the LM while\nalternating the planning and layout-to-audio tasks. The development\nlayout-to-audio loss determines checkpoint selection.\n\nSetting\n\nStage 1\n\nStage 2\n\nTasks\n\nLayout planning\n\nLayout planning and layout-to-audio\n\nEpoch limit\n\n10\n\n100\n\nLearning rate\n\nMonitored development task\n\nPlanning\n\nLayout-to-audio\n\nEarly-stopping patience\n\n5\n\n10\n\nTrainable scope\n\nNew embedding/LM-head rows\n\nLM\n\nLoss-bearing targets\n\nLayout span\n\nLayout or audio span for the sampled task\n\nTable 8: Stage-specific optimization protocol. The best Stage-2 checkpoint is\nrestored according to development layout-to-audio loss.\n\nTraining used six 80-GB GPUs, a maximum sequence length of 4,096, per-rank\nbatch size 2, gradient accumulation 4, and effective batch size 48. Parameters\nand AdamW [21] optimizer states remained FP32, while\nFSDP [41] computation used BF16.\nFull-shard FSDP used original parameters, automatic wrapping, synchronized\nmodule states, limited all-gathers, and replicated vocabulary parameters for\nthe Stage-1 row masks. The warmup ratio was 0.05 and the training random seed\nwas 42. The data split used seed 20260501.\n\nC.2 Matched-Data Controls\n\nThe no-layout ACE-Step 1.5-FT control uses the same filtered training items,\naudio codes, backbone LM, optimization precision, and adaptation budget as the\nfull model, but directly predicts audio tokens without a MusicLayout prefix.\nThe shuffled-layout-training control retains the MusicLayout-shaped prefix but\npairs each audio target with a layout from another item during training. At\ninference it generates a layout from the prompt and then generates audio, as in\nthe full generated-layout condition. The shuffled-layout-inference control\nuses the normally trained MusicLayout model but replaces the item-matched\nreference layout with a valid layout from another item. Thus, the three controls\nremove layout information, disrupt training-time correspondence, or disrupt\ninference-time correspondence, respectively.\n\nBoth shuffled conditions use seed 20260711. For shuffled-layout training, the\n24,474 training layouts form a one-to-one permutation of the same training\nitems, with zero fixed points. The 2,719 development items remain unchanged.\nFor shuffled-layout inference, FreeMIDI and MidiCaps are permuted independently\nwithin their evaluation sets. This yields 3,759 unique target–donor pairs and\nzero fixed points, preventing cross-dataset donors and self-matches. Donor\nlayouts are truncated or extended at segment boundaries to end at the target\nitem’s requested duration.\n\nAppendix D Baseline Selection and Exact Configurations\n\nD.1 Selection Scope\n\nWe restricted the baseline comparison to models with publicly released weights\nand executable inference implementations. We further required compatibility\nwith instrumental prompt-to-music generation and several-minute output, either\nthrough native variable-duration synthesis or through an established\ncontinuation mechanism. These criteria allow the reported systems to consume\nthe same prompts and target durations and to be evaluated using the same audio\nand structural metrics.\n\nAudioLDM 2 [20] and\nMustango [23] were considered but not included in the\nfinal comparison. Their released checkpoints and public inference protocols\nare centered on short clips and do not provide a validated overlapping-context\ncontinuation procedure for the several-minute setting used here. Concatenating\nindependently generated diffusion samples would introduce a different and\npotentially discontinuous generation procedure. In contrast, the reported\ndiffusion baseline, Stable Audio 3 Medium [10], supports the required duration\nnatively.\n\nD.2 Executed Configurations\n\nTable 9 reports the configurations used in the\nfinal comparison. Each system targets the item’s manifest duration.\nACE-Step-family and Stable Audio systems generate that duration natively,\nwithout concatenating independently generated clips. The corresponding model\nfamilies are described by Gong et al. [13] and Evans et al. [10].\n\nSystem\n\nCheckpoint/configuration\n\nExecuted inference settings\n\nMusicGen-Large\n\nfacebook/musicgen-large\n\n30-s window, 10-s audio context, 20-s retained hop, 51.2 tokens/s, 1,536 first-window and 1,024 continuation tokens, base seed 28602\n\nACE-Step 1.5\n\nacestep-v15-turbo, acestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B\n\n8 inference steps, LM temperature/top- 0.9/0.95, native target duration, base seed 38602\n\nACE-Step 1.5 XL-Turbo\n\nacestep-v15-xl-turbo, acestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B\n\n8 infe\n", "numLines": 1527, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1527}}}
